00:02:23.850 --> 00:02:26.460
What occurs in the mind
body when you have ADHD?

00:02:26.460 --> 00:02:29.145
Are there ways to address
it without medication?

00:02:29.145 --> 00:02:30.460
Thank you for this question.

00:02:30.460 --> 00:02:32.760
So attention deficit
hyperactivity disorder

00:02:32.760 --> 00:02:35.100
used to be called ADD.

00:02:35.100 --> 00:02:37.230
The hyperactivity part
is a little misleading.

00:02:37.230 --> 00:02:39.480
And again I'm not
a clinician here.

00:02:39.480 --> 00:02:43.440
Here's what we know
works for some people,

00:02:43.440 --> 00:02:44.940
and yet there are
always going to be

00:02:44.940 --> 00:02:49.980
side effects of any kind of
chemical manipulation, which

00:02:49.980 --> 00:02:54.270
is that, we know that people,
kids, and adults with ADHD

00:02:54.270 --> 00:02:57.810
actually have a tremendous
capacity to focus if they like

00:02:57.810 --> 00:02:58.950
what they're focusing on.

00:02:58.950 --> 00:03:01.928
You take a kid with
ADHD who can't focus

00:03:01.928 --> 00:03:03.720
and you give them their
favorite video game

00:03:03.720 --> 00:03:06.510
and they are a laser.

00:03:06.510 --> 00:03:10.380
The threshold to access the
dopamine system is higher.

00:03:10.380 --> 00:03:12.750
And dopamine has this
incredible ability

00:03:12.750 --> 00:03:17.850
to focus the brain and other
aspects of the nervous system.

00:03:17.850 --> 00:03:20.280
Certainly, if people
require medication,

00:03:20.280 --> 00:03:23.550
I'm not going to tell you to
stop taking that medication.

00:03:23.550 --> 00:03:28.560
But the focus training exercises
that have been explored mainly

00:03:28.560 --> 00:03:32.100
in China, but they're starting
to be explored over here

00:03:32.100 --> 00:03:34.930
as well, do seem
to be of benefit.

00:03:34.930 --> 00:03:36.460
And these are as they sound.

00:03:36.460 --> 00:03:39.900
They use them in schools in
China now, which are literally

00:03:39.900 --> 00:03:41.460
visual focus exercises.

00:03:41.460 --> 00:03:44.130
Your mental focus,
that is your ability

00:03:44.130 --> 00:03:47.880
to focus on things cognitively,
follows your visual focus.

00:03:47.880 --> 00:03:51.180
And of course your
stress will anchor your--

00:03:51.180 --> 00:03:53.620
essentially put you in a
soda straw view of the world.

00:03:53.620 --> 00:03:56.880
So yes, there are non
medication-based treatments.

00:03:56.880 --> 00:03:59.790
By medication I'm assuming you
mean prescription medication.

00:03:59.790 --> 00:04:02.280
There are of course
supplement-based medications

00:04:02.280 --> 00:04:05.580
that will increase
dopamine mainly L-tyrosine.

00:04:05.580 --> 00:04:07.640
Again, this is
something to think

00:04:07.640 --> 00:04:09.390
carefully about before
you start tampering

00:04:09.390 --> 00:04:10.473
with your dopamine system.

00:04:10.473 --> 00:04:11.190
But it is the--

00:04:11.190 --> 00:04:15.160
L-tyrosine is the
precursor to dopamine.

00:04:15.160 --> 00:04:17.370
So it will raise
your dopamine levels.

00:04:17.370 --> 00:04:20.519
But I believe, and
you'll hear me say this

00:04:20.519 --> 00:04:25.210
as many times as necessary,
that one should--

00:04:25.210 --> 00:04:28.540
if you can rely on
behavioral tools first,

00:04:28.540 --> 00:04:34.223
then of course, sleep and
nutritionists are prerequisite.

00:04:34.223 --> 00:04:36.640
Again, for all mental health,
physical health performance,

00:04:36.640 --> 00:04:37.932
you simply can't neglect those.

00:04:37.932 --> 00:04:41.440
And then and only
then if all of that

00:04:41.440 --> 00:04:44.200
isn't working to rely on
supplement-based tools

00:04:44.200 --> 00:04:47.030
or on prescription medication.

00:04:47.030 --> 00:04:52.600
So it's clear that Vyvanse,
Adderall, Ritalin, et cetera

00:04:52.600 --> 00:04:55.510
work for ADHD, but
some people choose

00:04:55.510 --> 00:04:58.450
to rely on more subtle forms
of pharmacologic manipulation

00:04:58.450 --> 00:04:59.410
like L-tyrosine.

00:04:59.410 --> 00:05:02.800
And this focusing
exercise essentially

00:05:02.800 --> 00:05:05.140
consists of spending
1 to 3 minutes trying

00:05:05.140 --> 00:05:06.273
to maintain visual focus.

00:05:06.273 --> 00:05:07.690
And yes, you are
allowed to blink.

00:05:07.690 --> 00:05:09.640
I don't know why we tend
to stare at something

00:05:09.640 --> 00:05:10.450
we don't blink.

00:05:10.450 --> 00:05:12.430
But don't let your eyes dry out.

00:05:12.430 --> 00:05:16.420
And that can increase your
ability to focus cognitively.

00:05:16.420 --> 00:05:17.740
And it works.

00:05:17.740 --> 00:05:21.760
And keep in mind that focusing
always involves a refocusing.

00:05:21.760 --> 00:05:25.270
We covered a beautiful data
set, not collected by my lab

00:05:25.270 --> 00:05:29.140
but by Wendy Suzuki's Lab at
NYU, that at roughly 10-minute,

00:05:29.140 --> 00:05:32.080
it's actually 13-minute a
day meditation of the sort,

00:05:32.080 --> 00:05:34.060
where you just focus
on your breathing,

00:05:34.060 --> 00:05:37.180
has been shown to improve
focus significantly.

00:05:37.180 --> 00:05:38.800
Why don't we hear
about this more?

00:05:38.800 --> 00:05:40.960
Well she's now Dean of
Arts and Sciences at NYU

00:05:40.960 --> 00:05:42.890
and all the students
are hearing about it.

00:05:42.890 --> 00:05:43.750
Hopefully, they're doing it.

00:05:43.750 --> 00:05:45.375
But it takes a little
bit of discipline

00:05:45.375 --> 00:05:48.880
for some reason 10-minute
a day type meditation

00:05:48.880 --> 00:05:51.250
is something that very few
people follow consistently.

00:05:51.250 --> 00:05:54.310
But if you're looking for non
medication-based treatments

00:05:54.310 --> 00:05:57.010
for ADHD, or you're somebody
who just struggles with focus,

00:05:57.010 --> 00:06:00.100
the focusing exercise
or the meditation,

00:06:00.100 --> 00:06:05.338
I just subscribed it can be
very useful, so say the data.

00:06:05.338 --> 00:06:07.380
Yeah, thanks for bringing
up space-time bridging.

00:06:07.380 --> 00:06:09.588
Are people familiar with
what space-time bridging is?

00:06:09.588 --> 00:06:11.140
I haven't talked a lot about it.

00:06:11.140 --> 00:06:13.300
OK, this is-- thanks
for bringing that up.

00:06:13.300 --> 00:06:15.300
We actually have an episode
on meditation coming

00:06:15.300 --> 00:06:16.342
up soon where I cover it.

00:06:16.342 --> 00:06:18.240
And I talked about
it long ago and then

00:06:18.240 --> 00:06:22.980
I kind of abandoned it because
well, we wanted more data

00:06:22.980 --> 00:06:25.810
and it's a pretty
interesting technique.

00:06:25.810 --> 00:06:29.235
If you think about the
nervous system and vision

00:06:29.235 --> 00:06:31.860
in particular, but if you're not
a sighted person or you're low

00:06:31.860 --> 00:06:34.710
vision, or no vision, you could
do this with your hearing.

00:06:34.710 --> 00:06:38.550
But I'm going to assume most
people here are sighted.

00:06:38.550 --> 00:06:41.280
If not just translate this
to the auditory system.

00:06:41.280 --> 00:06:43.748
You have this incredible
ability to close your eyes

00:06:43.748 --> 00:06:46.290
and focus, for instance, on--
people talk about the third eye

00:06:46.290 --> 00:06:48.450
center, focusing right
behind your forehead.

00:06:48.450 --> 00:06:52.530
Do you know why people do
that when they meditate?

00:06:52.530 --> 00:06:54.210
The reason is that
you actually have

00:06:54.210 --> 00:06:56.260
no sensation in your brain.

00:06:56.260 --> 00:07:00.090
It's the one place to focus
your attention for which

00:07:00.090 --> 00:07:03.650
you abandon sensation.

00:07:03.650 --> 00:07:06.328
If I think about any portion
of my body or my breathing,

00:07:06.328 --> 00:07:08.120
either I'm going to
sense what's happening.

00:07:08.120 --> 00:07:10.670
I'm going to perceive
my inner landscape,

00:07:10.670 --> 00:07:13.490
so-called interior
reception, or my outer.

00:07:13.490 --> 00:07:15.830
If I look out into the world,
it's external perception.

00:07:15.830 --> 00:07:18.560
When you focus your attention
with your eyes closed,

00:07:18.560 --> 00:07:21.470
you do have to close your eyes
just behind your forehead.

00:07:21.470 --> 00:07:26.550
You are focusing on your
thinking Sort of obvious,

00:07:26.550 --> 00:07:29.310
but I don't-- at least to me it
never been stated that clearly.

00:07:29.310 --> 00:07:32.100
Again, one of the
problems with some

00:07:32.100 --> 00:07:35.550
of the more traditional
practices but also the problem

00:07:35.550 --> 00:07:38.700
with science, is that there's a
shrouding of everything in very

00:07:38.700 --> 00:07:42.180
complex language, which sucks.

00:07:42.180 --> 00:07:42.900
Why is it suck?

00:07:42.900 --> 00:07:44.093
Because it's a separator.

00:07:44.093 --> 00:07:45.510
You eliminate the
number of people

00:07:45.510 --> 00:07:48.210
that can be brought to
potentially useful practices.

00:07:48.210 --> 00:07:51.960
And I don't like it when
people, including myself,

00:07:51.960 --> 00:07:57.360
overuse mechanism and
descriptions of fancy phrases

00:07:57.360 --> 00:08:01.080
to mask basic principles,
so simplest language

00:08:01.080 --> 00:08:06.490
I think it tends to unify
people around the practices.

00:08:06.490 --> 00:08:09.930
So when you focus on this
so-called third eye center,

00:08:09.930 --> 00:08:12.390
or a spot right behind your
forehead or on your breath--

00:08:12.390 --> 00:08:13.932
it's a little tricky
with the breath,

00:08:13.932 --> 00:08:17.370
but when you focus on
your frontal cortex,

00:08:17.370 --> 00:08:18.810
there's nothing to sense.

00:08:18.810 --> 00:08:20.682
Because there's no
sensory neurons there.

00:08:20.682 --> 00:08:21.390
There's no touch.

00:08:21.390 --> 00:08:22.080
There's no pain.

00:08:22.080 --> 00:08:22.660
Nothing.

00:08:22.660 --> 00:08:25.428
That's why in these gory movies,
you can take the skull off

00:08:25.428 --> 00:08:27.970
and-- while in neurosurgeries
they're poking around in there,

00:08:27.970 --> 00:08:29.820
and the person's
playing a violin.

00:08:29.820 --> 00:08:32.025
Like no anesthetic
No anesthetic.

00:08:32.025 --> 00:08:33.150
Doesn't require anesthetic.

00:08:33.150 --> 00:08:34.649
There's no sensory neurons.

00:08:34.649 --> 00:08:37.340
You can't sense anything there.

00:08:37.340 --> 00:08:40.492
So space-time
bridging involves--

00:08:40.492 --> 00:08:42.409
it's essentially a
meditation, but it's really

00:08:42.409 --> 00:08:44.000
a perceptual exercise.

00:08:44.000 --> 00:08:46.400
I think that's where
we're going with this,

00:08:46.400 --> 00:08:49.550
is it starts by
closing your eyes

00:08:49.550 --> 00:08:52.610
and focusing on that
location for which there

00:08:52.610 --> 00:08:53.600
is no sensation.

00:08:53.600 --> 00:08:55.820
There's only thought.

00:08:55.820 --> 00:08:59.780
And then opening your eyes
and focusing on a location

00:08:59.780 --> 00:09:02.740
maybe about the
distance of your hand.

00:09:02.740 --> 00:09:05.440
And you focus also
on your breathing.

00:09:05.440 --> 00:09:08.313
So you sort of imagine a kind
of a tether between that.

00:09:08.313 --> 00:09:10.480
You can split your attention
to these two locations.

00:09:10.480 --> 00:09:12.230
You're thinking about
your body and you're

00:09:12.230 --> 00:09:14.500
thinking about a
location outside of you.

00:09:14.500 --> 00:09:16.600
And then while
continuing to think

00:09:16.600 --> 00:09:18.700
about your body,
so-called intersection,

00:09:18.700 --> 00:09:20.770
focus on your breathing,
you focus further out,

00:09:20.770 --> 00:09:22.720
and then further out,
and then further out.

00:09:22.720 --> 00:09:25.240
And then ultimately, you know
that little cartoon or meme

00:09:25.240 --> 00:09:27.340
where they're like, we're just
a little blue dot floating

00:09:27.340 --> 00:09:28.210
in a big universe.

00:09:28.210 --> 00:09:31.480
And like it's supposed to make
all your problems go away.

00:09:31.480 --> 00:09:33.700
It kind of works
because what you've done

00:09:33.700 --> 00:09:35.320
is you've expanded
your perception,

00:09:35.320 --> 00:09:37.570
and you go oh, yeah the stuff
that's happening in here

00:09:37.570 --> 00:09:39.490
is really important
when I'm focused

00:09:39.490 --> 00:09:41.470
on what's happening in here.

00:09:41.470 --> 00:09:44.290
But when I'm focused
on what's going on

00:09:44.290 --> 00:09:46.720
and the vastness of all
this, and we're just

00:09:46.720 --> 00:09:48.550
a little pale blue
dot and all that,

00:09:48.550 --> 00:09:50.740
it changes your perception.

00:09:50.740 --> 00:09:53.170
Not just your visual
perception obviously.

00:09:53.170 --> 00:09:55.493
Changing your visual
perception changes

00:09:55.493 --> 00:09:57.160
your cognitive
perception, which changes

00:09:57.160 --> 00:09:58.550
your emotional experience.

00:09:58.550 --> 00:10:01.840
So the space-time bridging
is a perceptual exercise

00:10:01.840 --> 00:10:04.450
where you step from
focusing internally

00:10:04.450 --> 00:10:06.478
to focusing externally
at a short distance, then

00:10:06.478 --> 00:10:08.770
a further distance, further
distance, further distance,

00:10:08.770 --> 00:10:10.780
and then trying to
imagine yourself

00:10:10.780 --> 00:10:11.980
in this larger landscape.

00:10:11.980 --> 00:10:14.740
It sounds very mystical but it's
actually very neurobiological.

00:10:14.740 --> 00:10:17.710
And it captures
something really amazing.

00:10:17.710 --> 00:10:19.480
Why is the T in
there-- the time?

00:10:19.480 --> 00:10:22.360
Space-time bridging,
because this is space?

00:10:22.360 --> 00:10:27.370
But time is in there because
when you focus in close,

00:10:27.370 --> 00:10:30.280
your slicing of time is finer.

00:10:30.280 --> 00:10:32.080
You notice the
subtle fluctuations

00:10:32.080 --> 00:10:35.230
in your breathing and things
that are happening up close.

00:10:35.230 --> 00:10:38.620
Whereas when you focus further
out, your perception of time

00:10:38.620 --> 00:10:41.470
actually changes, which
is why in panoramic vision

00:10:41.470 --> 00:10:42.070
we are calm.

00:10:42.070 --> 00:10:43.570
And when you think
about, we're just

00:10:43.570 --> 00:10:46.060
a pale blue dot and we
mostly only live to about 85

00:10:46.060 --> 00:10:48.790
or maybe 100 years old, and
then what's happening right now,

00:10:48.790 --> 00:10:51.730
my boss being a jerk, and
all that doesn't really

00:10:51.730 --> 00:10:53.950
matter because the
Earth is spinning

00:10:53.950 --> 00:10:55.370
and all that kind of stuff.

00:10:55.370 --> 00:10:56.448
Which is all true.

00:10:56.448 --> 00:10:58.490
And it's the stuff of
philosophy and mindfulness.

00:10:58.490 --> 00:10:59.530
And I think it's beautiful.

00:10:59.530 --> 00:11:00.947
What you're really
doing is you're

00:11:00.947 --> 00:11:04.090
changing your time perception by
changing your space perception.

00:11:04.090 --> 00:11:06.970
So space-time bridging
is very useful

00:11:06.970 --> 00:11:12.640
because most people get
locked at one step, one

00:11:12.640 --> 00:11:15.820
of these stations, especially
under conditions of stress.

00:11:15.820 --> 00:11:19.570
And people who have
trouble focusing-- and I'm

00:11:19.570 --> 00:11:22.712
glad you brought this up
in this context of ADHD,

00:11:22.712 --> 00:11:24.670
people have a hard time
focusing whether or not

00:11:24.670 --> 00:11:28.330
they have ADHD or not,
tend to skip back and forth

00:11:28.330 --> 00:11:31.345
between different
space-time domains,

00:11:31.345 --> 00:11:32.470
as we call them in science.

00:11:32.470 --> 00:11:34.660
So this is a simple
exercise that you can do.

00:11:34.660 --> 00:11:37.330
Focusing internally then
stepping out externally,

00:11:37.330 --> 00:11:38.770
and then stepping back in.

00:11:38.770 --> 00:11:42.040
All the while paying attention
to your inner landscape

00:11:42.040 --> 00:11:44.500
just simply by focusing
on your breathing.

00:11:44.500 --> 00:11:46.510
It's a tool that we're
still collecting data

00:11:46.510 --> 00:11:49.150
on in terms of its utility, but
people are already using it.

00:11:49.150 --> 00:11:51.820
And I don't think of
it as a meditation.

00:11:51.820 --> 00:11:53.980
I think of it as a
perceptual exercise.

00:11:53.980 --> 00:11:55.623
Thanks for asking that.

00:11:55.623 --> 00:11:57.040
As a teenager what
are five things

00:11:57.040 --> 00:11:58.998
you would recommend to
physically feel my best?

00:11:58.998 --> 00:12:01.720
I'm a 15-year-old surfer
who attends high school

00:12:01.720 --> 00:12:02.565
and plays soccer.

00:12:02.565 --> 00:12:04.690
It sounds like you're doing
a lot of things, right?

00:12:09.070 --> 00:12:12.040
To physically feel your best.

00:12:12.040 --> 00:12:16.000
So I'm going to grasp at some
context here that I'm not--

00:12:16.000 --> 00:12:17.980
that's not within reach.

00:12:17.980 --> 00:12:21.497
I'm assuming if you are
doing all these things you're

00:12:21.497 --> 00:12:23.330
hopefully doing a bunch
of other things too.

00:12:23.330 --> 00:12:24.940
And they're going
to be demands on you

00:12:24.940 --> 00:12:27.107
that you probably some of
them you don't want to do,

00:12:27.107 --> 00:12:29.350
school and things
like that, are going

00:12:29.350 --> 00:12:34.570
to have varying levels of
joy and delight and demand

00:12:34.570 --> 00:12:36.550
of things you don't want to do.

00:12:36.550 --> 00:12:39.710
I don't want to default always
to the simplest of tools.

00:12:39.710 --> 00:12:42.730
But I certainly think
that even as a 15-year-old

00:12:42.730 --> 00:12:44.958
if you're not already getting
lots and lots of sleep,

00:12:44.958 --> 00:12:46.000
that's going to be great.

00:12:46.000 --> 00:12:47.667
Tell your parents
that I said you should

00:12:47.667 --> 00:12:49.315
get lots and lots of sleep.

00:12:49.315 --> 00:12:51.190
provided you're not
sleeping through classes,

00:12:51.190 --> 00:12:52.960
I'm a professor after all.

00:12:52.960 --> 00:12:56.500
I couldn't tell you otherwise.

00:12:56.500 --> 00:13:00.550
I would say if I could travel
back in time as a 15-year-old,

00:13:00.550 --> 00:13:05.050
I would encourage you
to cultivate some sort

00:13:05.050 --> 00:13:06.670
of mindfulness practice.

00:13:06.670 --> 00:13:08.500
I know this sounds
a little cliche,

00:13:08.500 --> 00:13:12.100
but having some awareness
of your thinking

00:13:12.100 --> 00:13:13.720
about your thinking is good.

00:13:13.720 --> 00:13:15.850
But I'm actually
not going to say

00:13:15.850 --> 00:13:19.743
sit down and meditate for 10
minutes a day, or do NSDR.

00:13:19.743 --> 00:13:21.160
I'm actually not
going to tell you

00:13:21.160 --> 00:13:24.190
that I think given how
plastic your brain is,

00:13:24.190 --> 00:13:27.700
how much it's changing at
15, I would encourage you--

00:13:27.700 --> 00:13:31.030
and maybe you would set a timer
for this, to actually develop

00:13:31.030 --> 00:13:35.230
just a really keen awareness
of what stresses you out,

00:13:35.230 --> 00:13:39.520
what relaxes you, what
delights, you et cetera.

00:13:39.520 --> 00:13:43.300
And just to simply develop
an awareness of that,

00:13:43.300 --> 00:13:47.180
because those are your antennae.

00:13:47.180 --> 00:13:52.550
And I certainly had a
meditation practice as a youth,

00:13:52.550 --> 00:13:54.860
mostly given to me, because
I was a little haywire

00:13:54.860 --> 00:13:56.630
and I needed it.

00:13:56.630 --> 00:13:58.110
And it worked pretty well.

00:13:58.110 --> 00:14:00.740
But I think in
retrospect, what I

00:14:00.740 --> 00:14:05.940
wish I had developed was more
of a sense of how I navigated

00:14:05.940 --> 00:14:09.417
stress or things
and things I enjoyed

00:14:09.417 --> 00:14:10.500
and things I didn't enjoy.

00:14:10.500 --> 00:14:13.920
And I would just encourage you
to have a general awareness.

00:14:13.920 --> 00:14:16.770
Try and detect and
learn about what

00:14:16.770 --> 00:14:19.770
raises your adrenaline, what
raises your dopamine, what

00:14:19.770 --> 00:14:24.790
raises your serotonin, and then
start thinking about tools.

00:14:24.790 --> 00:14:27.030
But, again the
awareness is going

00:14:27.030 --> 00:14:30.000
to be very valuable and gosh,
as a 15-year-old you are

00:14:30.000 --> 00:14:34.560
in this amazing blessed period
of heightened neuroplasticity,

00:14:34.560 --> 00:14:36.330
should we all be so lucky.

00:14:36.330 --> 00:14:37.740
So enjoy it.

00:14:40.520 --> 00:14:42.677
Next question please.

00:14:42.677 --> 00:14:44.563
Clarity on adrenaline
regarding cold water.

00:14:44.563 --> 00:14:46.480
Should we wait to feel
the rise of adrenaline,

00:14:46.480 --> 00:14:48.640
the "get me out of here"
feeling and the fall of it

00:14:48.640 --> 00:14:49.597
before bailing?

00:14:49.597 --> 00:14:53.740
Yes, provided it
doesn't kill you.

00:14:53.740 --> 00:14:55.420
I don't want to say cold water.

00:14:55.420 --> 00:14:59.185
It's hard to kill yourself with
cold water, provide your head's

00:14:59.185 --> 00:15:00.310
above and you're breathing.

00:15:00.310 --> 00:15:06.550
But it's-- sorry, my podcast
producer's always like,

00:15:06.550 --> 00:15:08.800
"I can't help that."

00:15:08.800 --> 00:15:15.470
Anyway, it's a great tool.

00:15:15.470 --> 00:15:17.640
And different days
it'll feel different.

00:15:17.640 --> 00:15:21.930
So for instance, doing any kind
of adrenaline and deliberate

00:15:21.930 --> 00:15:24.800
cold exposure, or adrenaline
increasing activity early

00:15:24.800 --> 00:15:28.040
in the day, you might find
that you are more "resilient"

00:15:28.040 --> 00:15:28.937
than later.

00:15:28.937 --> 00:15:31.520
In other words, the wall like,
I really don't want to do this.

00:15:31.520 --> 00:15:33.740
This is actually
interesting for I think

00:15:33.740 --> 00:15:35.330
it extends beyond cold water.

00:15:35.330 --> 00:15:38.660
Let's say you really don't
want to do something.

00:15:38.660 --> 00:15:40.520
Pay attention to the
fact that maybe it's

00:15:40.520 --> 00:15:41.660
not the right thing to do.

00:15:41.660 --> 00:15:43.160
But assuming it's
something that you

00:15:43.160 --> 00:15:45.200
know you should do but
you don't want to do,

00:15:45.200 --> 00:15:49.650
you are already in the
first wall of adrenaline.

00:15:49.650 --> 00:15:51.420
You don't experience
it necessarily

00:15:51.420 --> 00:15:53.580
as heightened levels of stress.

00:15:53.580 --> 00:15:56.100
You might experience it as
heightened levels of fatigue

00:15:56.100 --> 00:15:58.770
or a hard time shifting on
that kind of activation state

00:15:58.770 --> 00:16:01.440
that's required to
move through the thing.

00:16:01.440 --> 00:16:07.237
But I do encourage you to
take advantage of that.

00:16:07.237 --> 00:16:09.570
And we have an episode coming
out tomorrow actually that

00:16:09.570 --> 00:16:11.862
answers questions like, should
you train if you're sick

00:16:11.862 --> 00:16:13.020
and what if you travelling.

00:16:13.020 --> 00:16:15.510
And there's context always.

00:16:15.510 --> 00:16:19.303
But I think that you
do want to experience.

00:16:19.303 --> 00:16:21.720
If you want to get the most
out of the cold water exposure

00:16:21.720 --> 00:16:24.925
and to be more specific the
adrenaline, then you want

00:16:24.925 --> 00:16:27.300
to get to that point of I
really want to get out of here,

00:16:27.300 --> 00:16:28.800
but I know I can stay in safely.

00:16:28.800 --> 00:16:30.600
But I really want
to get out of here.

00:16:30.600 --> 00:16:33.460
And it's a little
hard to explain,

00:16:33.460 --> 00:16:37.200
but there's just so much
learning in those short moments

00:16:37.200 --> 00:16:39.550
about where your mind goes.

00:16:39.550 --> 00:16:43.410
And this sounds very kind of
again, subjective and maybe

00:16:43.410 --> 00:16:44.940
a little wishy-washy.

00:16:44.940 --> 00:16:48.900
But you can realize great
things about yourself

00:16:48.900 --> 00:16:49.690
in those moments.

00:16:49.690 --> 00:16:52.880
You can find insight
in those moments.

00:16:52.880 --> 00:16:56.150
Also keep in mind that
the degree of discomfort,

00:16:56.150 --> 00:16:58.580
not just physical but
mental discomfort,

00:16:58.580 --> 00:17:03.463
is directly predictive of
the pain to pleasure wave

00:17:03.463 --> 00:17:04.880
that you'll
experience afterwards.

00:17:04.880 --> 00:17:07.220
The reason it feels so good
when you get out of the ice bath

00:17:07.220 --> 00:17:08.210
and you're showered off--

00:17:08.210 --> 00:17:09.627
I always do the
warm shower after.

00:17:09.627 --> 00:17:11.750
I don't do this
end on cold thing.

00:17:11.750 --> 00:17:14.099
I don't know, It just
seems a little too painful.

00:17:14.099 --> 00:17:17.420
And then take a warm shower
and then you feel great.

00:17:17.420 --> 00:17:19.369
And that's the surge
of dopamine that we

00:17:19.369 --> 00:17:21.500
know based on a paper
published in the European

00:17:21.500 --> 00:17:25.849
Journal of physiology lasts many
hours, and it's a 100% to 200%

00:17:25.849 --> 00:17:27.890
increase in dopamine.

00:17:27.890 --> 00:17:29.600
It's not a subtle effect.

00:17:29.600 --> 00:17:31.513
And then people say,
wait, is that dopamine

00:17:31.513 --> 00:17:32.930
going to crash my
dopamine system?

00:17:32.930 --> 00:17:35.100
No, because it's
a nice slow rise.

00:17:35.100 --> 00:17:38.840
In fact, I am actually not aware
of many things besides love

00:17:38.840 --> 00:17:43.970
and delight that can
create this long, slow arc

00:17:43.970 --> 00:17:46.100
of dopamine lasting many hours.

00:17:46.100 --> 00:17:47.570
Maybe you're aware
of other things.

00:17:47.570 --> 00:17:48.840
If you are, let me know.

00:17:48.840 --> 00:17:54.920
But it turns out that long
arc is a true anti depressant.

00:17:54.920 --> 00:17:57.860
And my colleague at Stanford,
Dr. Anna Lembke who's

00:17:57.860 --> 00:18:00.650
the head of our Dual
Diagnosis Addiction Clinic

00:18:00.650 --> 00:18:03.710
has talked about in her
amazing book Dopamine Nation

00:18:03.710 --> 00:18:07.040
about patients of hers that
have really helped themselves

00:18:07.040 --> 00:18:11.360
along and out of the more
depressive phases of working

00:18:11.360 --> 00:18:14.000
through addiction and in
just depression in general

00:18:14.000 --> 00:18:17.000
through directed
cold water therapy.

00:18:17.000 --> 00:18:19.400
So I'm obviously a fanatic
about it in the sense

00:18:19.400 --> 00:18:23.360
that it's a powerful relatively
safe, if done properly.

00:18:23.360 --> 00:18:25.760
Safe, if done properly,
way to modulate

00:18:25.760 --> 00:18:27.800
your internal dopamine.

00:18:27.800 --> 00:18:29.300
Hopefully, I answered
your question.

00:18:29.300 --> 00:18:30.380
Next question please.

00:18:32.960 --> 00:18:35.840
Sorry, I caught it raised it
went off the fall, as well.

00:18:35.840 --> 00:18:39.320
Yes, I think you should
get out once you've

00:18:39.320 --> 00:18:40.490
accomplished something.

00:18:40.490 --> 00:18:43.702
Don't get out when you panic
unless it's dangerous, sorry.

00:18:43.702 --> 00:18:46.160
How can you train your brain
to feel more confident moments

00:18:46.160 --> 00:18:49.235
where you tend to
feel intimidated?

00:18:49.235 --> 00:18:53.750
OK, these are hard question.

00:18:53.750 --> 00:18:57.320
Because context is tricky
here, because I don't know what

00:18:57.320 --> 00:18:59.000
the context is.

00:18:59.000 --> 00:19:02.660
And confidence on short
time scales, and then

00:19:02.660 --> 00:19:04.700
long time scales, so
confidence in school,

00:19:04.700 --> 00:19:08.930
confidence in career,
those are long arc things.

00:19:08.930 --> 00:19:12.500
Whereas confidence to be able to
do something in the short term

00:19:12.500 --> 00:19:13.470
is different.

00:19:13.470 --> 00:19:15.620
But remember those
action sequences

00:19:15.620 --> 00:19:18.080
that trigger the
release of dopamine.

00:19:18.080 --> 00:19:20.210
I've mainly talked about
the dark side of dopamine,

00:19:20.210 --> 00:19:23.300
but I hopefully also talked
about the sort of upward spiral

00:19:23.300 --> 00:19:25.130
that dopamine can cause,
mainly by thinking

00:19:25.130 --> 00:19:28.130
about delight and things
that you really enjoy.

00:19:28.130 --> 00:19:30.560
That carries over.

00:19:30.560 --> 00:19:36.170
And I would say that
you want to micro slice

00:19:36.170 --> 00:19:41.240
the demands of what's maybe got
you back on your heels a bit.

00:19:41.240 --> 00:19:43.820
Actually a good friend of
mine who's here tonight,

00:19:43.820 --> 00:19:45.830
I think also my
friend Pat, he has

00:19:45.830 --> 00:19:49.520
a great way of
conceptualizing this, which

00:19:49.520 --> 00:19:52.430
is for most all
endeavors we either

00:19:52.430 --> 00:19:54.530
feel back on our heels,
flat footed, or forward

00:19:54.530 --> 00:19:55.310
center of mass.

00:19:55.310 --> 00:19:57.460
Like we can really do
something, we're flat footed,

00:19:57.460 --> 00:19:58.460
we're back on our heels.

00:19:58.460 --> 00:20:00.335
And sometimes getting
from back on our heels,

00:20:00.335 --> 00:20:03.740
lets call that lack of
confidence to just on two feet,

00:20:03.740 --> 00:20:06.770
and confident enough to move
forward or at least stay

00:20:06.770 --> 00:20:09.090
in the game.

00:20:09.090 --> 00:20:13.580
That's going to require you
could lean on different tools.

00:20:13.580 --> 00:20:16.600
I can't say which would be
ideal for the circumstance you

00:20:16.600 --> 00:20:17.600
have in mind.

00:20:17.600 --> 00:20:21.070
But I do think that having
a way to calm yourself

00:20:21.070 --> 00:20:27.000
will give you access to
more internal resources.

00:20:27.000 --> 00:20:27.560
We know this.

00:20:27.560 --> 00:20:29.280
This was something
I meant to bring up

00:20:29.280 --> 00:20:31.950
during the discussion
about fear versus love,

00:20:31.950 --> 00:20:35.130
et cetera, trying to
access delight and love.

00:20:35.130 --> 00:20:40.020
When we are in a state of
fear or stress or anxiety,

00:20:40.020 --> 00:20:44.550
the rule set, the options
available to us, and indeed

00:20:44.550 --> 00:20:47.700
our creativity, is
greatly diminished.

00:20:47.700 --> 00:20:50.532
And this has to do with the
way that the prefrontal cortex

00:20:50.532 --> 00:20:51.990
interacts with an
area of the brain

00:20:51.990 --> 00:20:55.110
called the insula, which relates
to our internal landscape.

00:20:55.110 --> 00:20:57.070
And there's this
weird phenomenon,

00:20:57.070 --> 00:20:59.460
which is that
normally, our brain--

00:20:59.460 --> 00:21:03.000
our thinking brain and our
rule setting brain can--

00:21:03.000 --> 00:21:08.890
it leads the brain parts that
control and pay attention

00:21:08.890 --> 00:21:10.240
to how we feel internally.

00:21:10.240 --> 00:21:12.250
And that's why for instance,
if you feel a little nervous

00:21:12.250 --> 00:21:13.450
you can still do something.

00:21:13.450 --> 00:21:15.730
At some point you
get stressed enough.

00:21:15.730 --> 00:21:19.690
And we know this from work by
my colleague David Spiegel,

00:21:19.690 --> 00:21:20.980
it reverses.

00:21:20.980 --> 00:21:22.628
And these areas
of the brain that

00:21:22.628 --> 00:21:24.670
are paying attention like
how flushed my face is,

00:21:24.670 --> 00:21:26.670
or whether or not I'm
sweating, or my breathing,

00:21:26.670 --> 00:21:30.950
actually start to shut down
creative decision making.

00:21:30.950 --> 00:21:32.890
So I would say the way
to have more confidence

00:21:32.890 --> 00:21:35.290
is to learn to
control that stress

00:21:35.290 --> 00:21:37.130
and keep the part of your brain.

00:21:37.130 --> 00:21:39.640
The prefrontal
cortex is that part

00:21:39.640 --> 00:21:43.630
that can come up with
new rules that can

00:21:43.630 --> 00:21:47.380
be funny that can be creative.

00:21:47.380 --> 00:21:49.702
That keeps that
brain part leading.

00:21:49.702 --> 00:21:51.910
The way to think about this
is, the prefrontal cortex

00:21:51.910 --> 00:21:53.560
is sort of like the coach,
and the rest of your brain

00:21:53.560 --> 00:21:54.768
are sort of like the players.

00:21:54.768 --> 00:21:57.843
And if you get too stressed, the
players start to lead the game

00:21:57.843 --> 00:22:00.010
and the coach follows, and
kind of drags them along.

00:22:00.010 --> 00:22:02.980
So I would encourage you to
focus on real-time stress

00:22:02.980 --> 00:22:05.380
modulation, and to
raise your stress

00:22:05.380 --> 00:22:08.740
threshold using the sorts
of tools we talked about.

00:22:08.740 --> 00:22:10.860
And to register your wins.

00:22:10.860 --> 00:22:12.610
I didn't get into this
in too much detail,

00:22:12.610 --> 00:22:15.910
but one of the amazing things
about the dopamine system

00:22:15.910 --> 00:22:20.770
is that it's highly subject
to your interpretation.

00:22:20.770 --> 00:22:25.240
If you tell yourself
that a fail was a win,

00:22:25.240 --> 00:22:28.480
and you can see or conceptualize
some way in which that's

00:22:28.480 --> 00:22:31.682
actually true, you get to
tap into the dopamine system.

00:22:31.682 --> 00:22:32.890
You might think that's crazy.

00:22:32.890 --> 00:22:34.210
You can cheat your own brain.

00:22:34.210 --> 00:22:35.793
You can cheat your
own neurochemistry.

00:22:35.793 --> 00:22:36.770
And indeed you can.

00:22:36.770 --> 00:22:39.820
You can change the time,
space-time reference.

00:22:39.820 --> 00:22:43.220
And we see this with examples
like Nelson Mandela or Viktor

00:22:43.220 --> 00:22:43.720
Frankl.

00:22:43.720 --> 00:22:49.180
You read their stories trapped
in little cells, confined,

00:22:49.180 --> 00:22:50.170
imprisoned.

00:22:50.170 --> 00:22:53.140
And they come up with new ways
to access the dopamine system

00:22:53.140 --> 00:22:55.883
by now not thinking about
what they're not getting,

00:22:55.883 --> 00:22:57.550
but thinking about
what they can control

00:22:57.550 --> 00:22:58.960
in their immediate experience.

00:22:58.960 --> 00:23:03.920
Many examples of this throughout
literature and history.

00:23:03.920 --> 00:23:08.510
And the dopamine system
is the life force system.

00:23:08.510 --> 00:23:11.090
I don't say that
in any loose way.

00:23:11.090 --> 00:23:13.850
Dopamine is life force.

00:23:13.850 --> 00:23:16.760
It's the wish and the
desire to continue.

00:23:16.760 --> 00:23:19.540
It's persistence.

00:23:19.540 --> 00:23:24.670
And so if you can think about
what might seem like a failure

00:23:24.670 --> 00:23:26.560
and really spend
some time thinking

00:23:26.560 --> 00:23:29.650
about not the potential
wins on the outside,

00:23:29.650 --> 00:23:32.020
but how you can conceptualize
that as a potential win

00:23:32.020 --> 00:23:35.860
internally, you really
do get to achieve

00:23:35.860 --> 00:23:38.530
an internal chemical win.

00:23:38.530 --> 00:23:43.320
And that chemical win sets
you up for more real wins,

00:23:43.320 --> 00:23:44.320
hopeful that make sense.

00:23:44.320 --> 00:23:46.670
It's incredible how
contextualize the dopamine

00:23:46.670 --> 00:23:47.170
system is.

00:23:47.170 --> 00:23:49.360
But if it weren't,
why would it matter

00:23:49.360 --> 00:23:53.320
if we're talking about money or
mates or food or job or school?

00:23:53.320 --> 00:23:57.070
You don't get 50 reward
systems and motivation systems.

00:23:57.070 --> 00:23:59.470
You get one, and that's
the dopamine system.

00:24:02.310 --> 00:24:03.630
Next question please.

00:24:03.630 --> 00:24:07.020
What is the competing mechanism
behind bilateral eye movement,

00:24:07.020 --> 00:24:10.230
EMDR, that helps resolve
psychological trauma?

00:24:10.230 --> 00:24:11.940
The competing mechanism.

00:24:14.490 --> 00:24:17.383
Well, let me try and
answer as best I can.

00:24:17.383 --> 00:24:19.800
I'm not sure I understand the
full extent of the question.

00:24:19.800 --> 00:24:21.780
But let me--

00:24:21.780 --> 00:24:25.850
EMDR, moving your eyes
from side to side, right?

00:24:25.850 --> 00:24:28.280
And then recounting a
trauma is a very common

00:24:28.280 --> 00:24:31.370
and actually one of the four
approved treatments that

00:24:31.370 --> 00:24:33.690
are behavioral for trauma.

00:24:33.690 --> 00:24:37.520
So it's taken seriously in the
psychiatric and psychological

00:24:37.520 --> 00:24:39.090
community for good reason.

00:24:39.090 --> 00:24:42.170
It tends to work best for
single event traumas as opposed

00:24:42.170 --> 00:24:44.360
to entire childhoods.

00:24:44.360 --> 00:24:45.380
No joke there.

00:24:45.380 --> 00:24:47.838
Some people have their entire
childhood that was traumatic.

00:24:47.838 --> 00:24:51.320
Other people they experience
a single event trauma

00:24:51.320 --> 00:24:58.070
or repeated periods of the
same or similar type of trauma.

00:24:58.070 --> 00:24:59.810
Eye movements from
side to side have

00:24:59.810 --> 00:25:01.580
been shown in a
number of studies

00:25:01.580 --> 00:25:04.768
to very potently reduce
the activity of a brain

00:25:04.768 --> 00:25:07.310
structure called the amygdala,
which most people are familiar

00:25:07.310 --> 00:25:10.040
with, because of the character
from the Star Wars movie,

00:25:10.040 --> 00:25:12.890
Amygdala.

00:25:12.890 --> 00:25:17.180
There's a neuroscientist
somewhere on that team.

00:25:17.180 --> 00:25:19.280
It is indeed a threat
detection center

00:25:19.280 --> 00:25:23.610
and when you move through
space, not outer space,

00:25:23.610 --> 00:25:25.820
but when you walk like
this your eyes actually

00:25:25.820 --> 00:25:28.220
generate these subtle
side to side shifts,

00:25:28.220 --> 00:25:30.980
unless you're focusing
on a specific target.

00:25:30.980 --> 00:25:33.500
And my lab and
other laboratories

00:25:33.500 --> 00:25:38.720
have found that leads to a very
potent quieting of the threat

00:25:38.720 --> 00:25:39.980
detection system.

00:25:39.980 --> 00:25:42.440
And then EMDR is
essentially a process

00:25:42.440 --> 00:25:46.220
of pairing that calmer state
with no threat detection

00:25:46.220 --> 00:25:49.670
system activated with the
recount of something that

00:25:49.670 --> 00:25:51.440
normally would be
quite triggering.

00:25:51.440 --> 00:25:53.540
So you've heard of
Pavlovian conditioning,

00:25:53.540 --> 00:25:58.070
like a bell rings and the
animal gets fed and animal

00:25:58.070 --> 00:26:00.920
salivate eventually, just the
bell will evoke the salivation.

00:26:00.920 --> 00:26:02.750
You're doing the
reverse of that.

00:26:02.750 --> 00:26:05.000
It's called behavioral
desensitization.

00:26:05.000 --> 00:26:07.220
It has an underlying
mechanism, et cetera.

00:26:07.220 --> 00:26:09.680
But the idea is to
pair a calm state

00:26:09.680 --> 00:26:11.870
with recount of something.

00:26:11.870 --> 00:26:14.130
It has been shown
to be successful.

00:26:14.130 --> 00:26:18.980
There are people who think that
the side to side eye movements

00:26:18.980 --> 00:26:21.170
and the recount of
trauma may actually be

00:26:21.170 --> 00:26:23.690
invoking some form of hypnosis.

00:26:23.690 --> 00:26:28.340
My colleague David Spiegel's
an expert in clinical hypnosis,

00:26:28.340 --> 00:26:30.830
has appeared on my
podcasts, Rituals podcasts

00:26:30.830 --> 00:26:31.940
and a few other podcasts.

00:26:31.940 --> 00:26:34.040
And talks about, this it
is not stage hypnosis,

00:26:34.040 --> 00:26:35.180
it's clinical hypnosis.

00:26:35.180 --> 00:26:37.640
So there may be
something going on there.

00:26:37.640 --> 00:26:40.490
EMDR-- again, some people
get great relief from it.

00:26:40.490 --> 00:26:41.390
Other people don't.

00:26:41.390 --> 00:26:43.760
What's kind of nice is
that this eye movements

00:26:43.760 --> 00:26:46.220
from side to side or simply
taking a walk as long as you're

00:26:46.220 --> 00:26:48.720
not looking at your phone and
not allowing your eyes to move

00:26:48.720 --> 00:26:51.470
from side to side,
is a very good way

00:26:51.470 --> 00:26:54.320
to shut down the fear
and stress system.

00:26:54.320 --> 00:26:57.040
So taking a walk I think is
relaxing for obvious reasons.

00:26:57.040 --> 00:26:58.555
And there are data
showing that part

00:26:58.555 --> 00:27:00.430
of the reason why animals
scratch at the door

00:27:00.430 --> 00:27:02.800
and want to go for a walk may
not actually be the exercise.

00:27:02.800 --> 00:27:05.080
There's kind of an anxiety
and then an anxiety relief

00:27:05.080 --> 00:27:06.620
that occurs.

00:27:06.620 --> 00:27:08.650
Of course, they have to
go to the bathroom too.

00:27:08.650 --> 00:27:10.450
One of Costello's
great joys in life

00:27:10.450 --> 00:27:15.850
was just peeing on everything
outdoors, thankfully.

00:27:15.850 --> 00:27:19.180
So the psychological trauma
rewiring, unfortunately there

00:27:19.180 --> 00:27:22.120
haven't been a lot of brain
imaging studies looking

00:27:22.120 --> 00:27:24.472
at this long term of
how well EMDR works.

00:27:24.472 --> 00:27:26.680
What I think is going to
happen in the next few years

00:27:26.680 --> 00:27:29.740
by the way, is it is not going
to be a discussion around,

00:27:29.740 --> 00:27:31.420
should you do
EMDR, should you do

00:27:31.420 --> 00:27:33.130
transcranial
magnetic stimulation,

00:27:33.130 --> 00:27:34.720
should you do
behavioral therapy?

00:27:34.720 --> 00:27:36.700
It's going to be
combination therapies.

00:27:36.700 --> 00:27:40.030
Combination therapies, including
pharmacologic manipulations

00:27:40.030 --> 00:27:44.350
to essentially give a
boost to the systems that

00:27:44.350 --> 00:27:47.680
encourage neuroplasticity,
like dopamine and serotonin and

00:27:47.680 --> 00:27:48.370
adrenaline.

00:27:48.370 --> 00:27:51.880
And then also then perform EMDR.

00:27:51.880 --> 00:27:53.620
And if you want to
talk about what's

00:27:53.620 --> 00:27:55.570
happening in the landscape
of clinical trials

00:27:55.570 --> 00:27:57.903
on some of the psychedelics,
I'm happy to talk about it.

00:27:57.903 --> 00:28:00.920
They're still
illegal, but they are

00:28:00.920 --> 00:28:02.170
being used in clinical trials.

00:28:02.170 --> 00:28:05.540
And very interesting
stuff is happening there.

00:28:05.540 --> 00:28:06.783
OK, next question please.

00:28:06.783 --> 00:28:08.200
What new research
or interventions

00:28:08.200 --> 00:28:09.825
are you most excited
about in the realm

00:28:09.825 --> 00:28:12.760
of health and wellness?

00:28:12.760 --> 00:28:16.610
So what I think is
going to be very

00:28:16.610 --> 00:28:19.190
interesting in the
next few years really

00:28:19.190 --> 00:28:21.740
reflects my memory
obsession that you've

00:28:21.740 --> 00:28:23.048
seen a little bit of tonight.

00:28:23.048 --> 00:28:25.340
But the thing that I think
is going to be most useful--

00:28:25.340 --> 00:28:27.800
and I've seen this
in science before

00:28:27.800 --> 00:28:30.590
and I think we're going to
see it in health and wellness.

00:28:30.590 --> 00:28:33.690
It's that there are all these
tools and all these people,

00:28:33.690 --> 00:28:36.750
and he's saying this
and she's saying that.

00:28:36.750 --> 00:28:39.230
But what, we're going to
start paying attention to

00:28:39.230 --> 00:28:41.045
is, what are the common themes?

00:28:44.190 --> 00:28:46.950
And a broader and
more important theme

00:28:46.950 --> 00:28:49.560
is going to be one of
modulation versus mediation.

00:28:49.560 --> 00:28:50.470
What do I mean?

00:28:50.470 --> 00:28:54.810
Well, if someone were to
pull a fire alarm right now,

00:28:54.810 --> 00:28:58.555
and please don't, that
will shift our attention

00:28:58.555 --> 00:29:00.430
and make it hard to
focus on what I'm saying.

00:29:00.430 --> 00:29:03.920
And knowing me I'd probably
just stay up here talking.

00:29:03.920 --> 00:29:08.090
Do we think that fire
alarms mediate attention?

00:29:08.090 --> 00:29:10.820
No, they modulate it.

00:29:10.820 --> 00:29:12.380
If it were very
cold in this room

00:29:12.380 --> 00:29:18.030
like it was when we first got
here tonight, or arctic cold--

00:29:18.030 --> 00:29:20.980
hopefully it's warmed up a bit.

00:29:20.980 --> 00:29:23.560
It has and I'm so sorry.

00:29:23.560 --> 00:29:25.340
So sorry.

00:29:25.340 --> 00:29:27.850
Yeah, I attempted to-- yeah.

00:29:27.850 --> 00:29:29.440
I almost thought
maybe we all just do

00:29:29.440 --> 00:29:31.960
a bunch of breathing to heat
up like adrenaline release.

00:29:31.960 --> 00:29:34.070
But these days getting
groups of people

00:29:34.070 --> 00:29:36.490
to all breathe on each
other is not exactly a--

00:29:36.490 --> 00:29:39.400
I can see that might go
the wrong way in terms

00:29:39.400 --> 00:29:42.790
of what people interpret.

00:29:42.790 --> 00:29:47.930
So the idea here is that
certain things directly

00:29:47.930 --> 00:29:49.310
mediate something.

00:29:49.310 --> 00:29:51.140
Like a physiological
side directly

00:29:51.140 --> 00:29:52.550
calms you down quickly.

00:29:52.550 --> 00:29:55.520
It mediates the
calming response.

00:29:55.520 --> 00:29:58.850
Getting good sleep makes
you less easily triggered.

00:29:58.850 --> 00:30:01.040
It modulates stress.

00:30:01.040 --> 00:30:04.670
But is sleeping directly
mediating stress control?

00:30:04.670 --> 00:30:07.170
No, and I think this
is really important.

00:30:07.170 --> 00:30:10.730
And this brings up the
topic of the gut-brain axis.

00:30:10.730 --> 00:30:14.420
The gut is rich with
these little bugs,

00:30:14.420 --> 00:30:15.920
bacteria trillions
of them, which

00:30:15.920 --> 00:30:17.330
is an eerie thought to me.

00:30:17.330 --> 00:30:20.030
But also the surface of your
skin, the surface of your eyes,

00:30:20.030 --> 00:30:22.160
you have a skin microbiome,
a nasal microbiome.

00:30:22.160 --> 00:30:24.890
Every mucosal lining
has a microbiome.

00:30:24.890 --> 00:30:28.360
In fact, think about this.

00:30:28.360 --> 00:30:31.000
This is a crazy but
worthwhile tangent.

00:30:31.000 --> 00:30:33.670
Have you ever bitten the
inside of your mouth?

00:30:33.670 --> 00:30:34.570
It sucks, right?

00:30:34.570 --> 00:30:35.980
And you get cut and it hurts.

00:30:35.980 --> 00:30:38.680
But guess what, the
inside of your mouth

00:30:38.680 --> 00:30:41.500
heals without a scar.

00:30:41.500 --> 00:30:42.430
Think about that.

00:30:42.430 --> 00:30:43.300
Weird, right?

00:30:43.300 --> 00:30:45.022
You cut anywhere
else on your body,

00:30:45.022 --> 00:30:46.480
and depending on
how well you heal,

00:30:46.480 --> 00:30:51.250
and your age and your immune
status, you get a scar.

00:30:51.250 --> 00:30:53.890
Your mouth is
filled with bacteria

00:30:53.890 --> 00:30:58.410
and it's open to the world,
but the gut microbiome,

00:30:58.410 --> 00:31:03.160
provided it's healthy,
provides an incredible ability

00:31:03.160 --> 00:31:05.040
to heal quickly.

00:31:05.040 --> 00:31:08.160
And I'm not somebody who's
done a lot of acupuncture.

00:31:08.160 --> 00:31:09.220
I went a few times.

00:31:09.220 --> 00:31:10.260
And now there's
interesting science

00:31:10.260 --> 00:31:11.190
happening on acupuncture.

00:31:11.190 --> 00:31:13.607
But what's the first thing
they do when you walk in there?

00:31:13.607 --> 00:31:15.246
Let me see your tongue.

00:31:15.246 --> 00:31:16.740
And then they go oh, yeah.

00:31:16.740 --> 00:31:18.360
And they have this
cool intuition

00:31:18.360 --> 00:31:20.940
that's not based on Western
mechanistic science.

00:31:20.940 --> 00:31:23.800
It's more of an intuition
based on millions,

00:31:23.800 --> 00:31:25.590
if not billions of
data points that have

00:31:25.590 --> 00:31:26.970
been put into these charts.

00:31:26.970 --> 00:31:28.170
It's pretty cool.

00:31:28.170 --> 00:31:30.327
And what they are
looking at, I believe

00:31:30.327 --> 00:31:32.910
and from what my colleagues who
work on microbiome tell me is,

00:31:32.910 --> 00:31:34.797
they can look at the
pallor of your tongue,

00:31:34.797 --> 00:31:36.630
in particular in the
back and get a sense of

00:31:36.630 --> 00:31:38.550
whether or not the
microbiome there

00:31:38.550 --> 00:31:42.520
is of the appropriate stuff.

00:31:42.520 --> 00:31:44.800
But they don't go,
oh, lactobacillus.

00:31:44.800 --> 00:31:48.177
Remember this all end
to illness, right?

00:31:48.177 --> 00:31:49.510
But then, Oh you're just biotic.

00:31:49.510 --> 00:31:50.980
Instead they get a sense.

00:31:50.980 --> 00:31:53.620
Now, parents of
small babies learn

00:31:53.620 --> 00:31:55.510
to detect all sorts
of things coming out

00:31:55.510 --> 00:31:57.580
of essentially every
orifice of the child

00:31:57.580 --> 00:31:59.590
as a readout of health
because the child doesn't

00:31:59.590 --> 00:32:00.640
have language.

00:32:00.640 --> 00:32:04.110
And a dog owners unfortunately
you want to do this too,

00:32:04.110 --> 00:32:05.110
for better or for worse.

00:32:05.110 --> 00:32:06.270
Probably for better, right?

00:32:06.270 --> 00:32:09.390
So we have this intuition
about gut health.

00:32:09.390 --> 00:32:12.870
But gut health would be
another example, where

00:32:12.870 --> 00:32:15.780
it's very clear now that
fiber can be helpful

00:32:15.780 --> 00:32:18.450
but it's mostly consuming
these fermented foods

00:32:18.450 --> 00:32:21.250
that have been used for ages.

00:32:21.250 --> 00:32:25.330
But low sugar fermented foods of
the natto, kimchi, sauerkraut,

00:32:25.330 --> 00:32:28.170
kombucha, et cetera,
all these things

00:32:28.170 --> 00:32:29.970
depending on which
culture you're in,

00:32:29.970 --> 00:32:32.190
they come in different forms.

00:32:32.190 --> 00:32:35.370
Certain yogurts, et cetera, that
allow the gut to be healthy.

00:32:35.370 --> 00:32:38.213
And it modulates a
huge number of systems.

00:32:38.213 --> 00:32:39.630
So I don't think
that you're going

00:32:39.630 --> 00:32:43.650
to cure depression by
adjusting your gut microbiome.

00:32:43.650 --> 00:32:49.350
But if your gut microbiota are
not well, and you improve that,

00:32:49.350 --> 00:32:52.140
it will indeed shift the
neurotransmitter systems

00:32:52.140 --> 00:32:55.500
of your brain and give
you a elevated mood.

00:32:55.500 --> 00:32:57.343
That shouldn't come
as a surprise anymore.

00:32:57.343 --> 00:32:59.760
But I think that the whole
world thinks like, gosh it must

00:32:59.760 --> 00:33:01.320
be the serotonin in the gut.

00:33:01.320 --> 00:33:03.630
No, it's actually
not serotonin gut.

00:33:03.630 --> 00:33:06.270
It's that the gut microbiota
create chemicals that actually

00:33:06.270 --> 00:33:10.710
become serotonin in the
brain, or become dopamine

00:33:10.710 --> 00:33:11.710
in the brain.

00:33:11.710 --> 00:33:13.780
And so I think that
the gut microbiome,

00:33:13.780 --> 00:33:15.180
I would put in
the same category,

00:33:15.180 --> 00:33:17.010
although not quite as important.

00:33:17.010 --> 00:33:19.020
I would put it in the
category of like sleep.

00:33:19.020 --> 00:33:21.600
It modulates a huge
number of other processes.

00:33:21.600 --> 00:33:26.340
It doesn't mediate them.
, So sunlight, sleep,

00:33:26.340 --> 00:33:30.820
healthy gut microbiome,
exercise, good nutrition,

00:33:30.820 --> 00:33:34.770
social connection, these things
all create this general milieu

00:33:34.770 --> 00:33:36.900
or environment of health.

00:33:36.900 --> 00:33:41.250
I would like to see more
distinction between modulating

00:33:41.250 --> 00:33:44.550
and mediating effects
and tools out there,

00:33:44.550 --> 00:33:47.880
because I also see a lot
of unnecessary argument.

00:33:47.880 --> 00:33:49.450
People are like,
there's no example

00:33:49.450 --> 00:33:52.363
that improving your gut
microbiome cures depression.

00:33:52.363 --> 00:33:53.280
Of course there's not.

00:33:53.280 --> 00:33:54.738
But there are really
good examples.

00:33:54.738 --> 00:33:57.450
If your gut microbiome
is off that improving

00:33:57.450 --> 00:33:59.632
it can improve mood,
which depending

00:33:59.632 --> 00:34:01.590
on where you are on that
spectrum of depression

00:34:01.590 --> 00:34:03.160
can really relieve things.

00:34:03.160 --> 00:34:05.700
So I think that the
future of health,

00:34:05.700 --> 00:34:07.605
we hear so much about
personalized medicine

00:34:07.605 --> 00:34:08.730
and matched to your genome.

00:34:08.730 --> 00:34:10.590
But we don't even
have the basic.

00:34:10.590 --> 00:34:13.139
Most people don't even
have the basics right.

00:34:13.139 --> 00:34:16.590
And if you watch or listen
to the podcast long enough,

00:34:16.590 --> 00:34:19.710
hopefully certain themes
start to repeat themselves.

00:34:19.710 --> 00:34:22.860
But a key theme that
you learn in science,

00:34:22.860 --> 00:34:26.370
you teach your students, does it
modulate or does it mediate it?

00:34:26.370 --> 00:34:28.409
You need to be careful
with your language there.

00:34:28.409 --> 00:34:32.250
And there's great information,
or as we say, interpretation

00:34:32.250 --> 00:34:33.570
power there.

00:34:33.570 --> 00:34:36.360
If you understand
the difference then

00:34:36.360 --> 00:34:39.989
I think we can go a long way
by making that distinction

00:34:39.989 --> 00:34:41.843
modulating versus mediating.

00:34:41.843 --> 00:34:44.010
There are probably other
things that modulate health

00:34:44.010 --> 00:34:45.659
that I'm overlooking
now just because

00:34:45.659 --> 00:34:49.690
of the flow that I'm in.

00:34:49.690 --> 00:34:51.100
The CoolMitt.

00:34:51.100 --> 00:34:52.870
Yeah, the CoolMitt.

00:34:52.870 --> 00:34:54.679
Palmer cooling.

00:34:54.679 --> 00:34:57.250
OK, I promised to talk
about Palmer cooling.

00:34:57.250 --> 00:34:58.210
Well, I'll do it now.

00:34:58.210 --> 00:35:01.000
Palmer cooling they
changed the Q&amp;A format.

00:35:01.000 --> 00:35:03.970
What can I say, this is like
teaching in the classroom.

00:35:03.970 --> 00:35:06.190
All right, very briefly.

00:35:06.190 --> 00:35:09.280
The Palmer cooling, which
is essentially placing--

00:35:09.280 --> 00:35:12.700
you can cool the core
of the body most quickly

00:35:12.700 --> 00:35:17.410
by placing cold objects on the
hands, the bottoms of the feet,

00:35:17.410 --> 00:35:19.060
or on the top of
the face, because

00:35:19.060 --> 00:35:21.460
of the arrangement
of vasculature.

00:35:21.460 --> 00:35:24.230
Normally, you've got this
arteries, capillaries,

00:35:24.230 --> 00:35:27.550
veins things, but at those
locations in the body,

00:35:27.550 --> 00:35:29.590
you skip the capillaries
and you can basically--

00:35:29.590 --> 00:35:31.390
you're not really
passing cooling the body,

00:35:31.390 --> 00:35:34.130
but you're cooling off the
core of the body more quickly.

00:35:34.130 --> 00:35:36.430
And, if you do that in
between sets of exercise

00:35:36.430 --> 00:35:39.460
or during a run or cycling
you can dramatically

00:35:39.460 --> 00:35:41.860
increase your
ability to continue.

00:35:41.860 --> 00:35:44.680
I actually use the CoolMitt
for cognitive work.

00:35:44.680 --> 00:35:46.330
But you don't need a CoolMitt.

00:35:46.330 --> 00:35:48.250
Sorry, guys.

00:35:48.250 --> 00:35:53.050
You can just get a thing of ice
water or just very cold water.

00:35:53.050 --> 00:35:55.090
And I know it sounds
trivially easy,

00:35:55.090 --> 00:35:57.970
but you're actually
just cooling your core

00:35:57.970 --> 00:36:01.840
by putting your hands or
even one hand on a relatively

00:36:01.840 --> 00:36:04.400
cold thing of water or ice.

00:36:04.400 --> 00:36:07.790
But not so cold that it
constricts the vasculature

00:36:07.790 --> 00:36:08.290
there.

00:36:08.290 --> 00:36:11.290
This is the incredible work of
my colleague at Stanford Dr.

00:36:11.290 --> 00:36:12.520
Craig Heller.

00:36:12.520 --> 00:36:14.860
Why wouldn't more people
do this if you can

00:36:14.860 --> 00:36:16.360
double the amount of endurance?

00:36:16.360 --> 00:36:17.110
Believe it or not.

00:36:17.110 --> 00:36:19.180
Or double the number
of sets of exercise

00:36:19.180 --> 00:36:22.990
you can do or feel more alert
and do more cognitive work.

00:36:22.990 --> 00:36:24.370
Why wouldn't more people do it?

00:36:24.370 --> 00:36:25.720
Because people just don't do it.

00:36:25.720 --> 00:36:27.700
And it sounds crazy.

00:36:27.700 --> 00:36:29.992
It really sounds crazy,
but it's a real thing.

00:36:29.992 --> 00:36:31.450
And I wish more
people would do it.

00:36:31.450 --> 00:36:33.160
The athletes at Stanford do it.

00:36:33.160 --> 00:36:34.430
People in the military do it.

00:36:34.430 --> 00:36:37.310
So people who know and
they use it, enjoy it.

00:36:37.310 --> 00:36:43.243
It's just it's almost like
seems to off target from what

00:36:43.243 --> 00:36:44.410
you're trying to accomplish.

00:36:44.410 --> 00:36:45.952
I don't know, for
some reason, people

00:36:45.952 --> 00:36:49.290
are finally onboard breathing
like in a specific way

00:36:49.290 --> 00:36:50.040
as a useful tool.

00:36:50.040 --> 00:36:52.922
A few years ago no
one was into that.

00:36:52.922 --> 00:36:54.630
I mean, just think of
how far we've come.

00:36:54.630 --> 00:36:55.950
It's incredible.

00:36:55.950 --> 00:36:58.650
People are talking about
psychedelics, meditation,

00:36:58.650 --> 00:36:59.340
breathing.

00:36:59.340 --> 00:37:02.370
I think the pandemic
for all its pains

00:37:02.370 --> 00:37:03.780
and, you know,
what a challenging

00:37:03.780 --> 00:37:07.080
period for all sorts of reasons,
did wake people up to the idea

00:37:07.080 --> 00:37:09.840
that you have to take
control over your health,

00:37:09.840 --> 00:37:13.350
because there's no magic
fairy coming to do it for you.

00:37:13.350 --> 00:37:16.620
And with all due respect there's
no government agency that's

00:37:16.620 --> 00:37:19.680
going to drop off the kit
at your front door of like,

00:37:19.680 --> 00:37:21.430
here's how you take
good care of yourself.

00:37:21.430 --> 00:37:24.370
So it just not going to happen.

00:37:24.370 --> 00:37:26.620
And it wouldn't happen
under any circumstances.

00:37:26.620 --> 00:37:30.090
So it's a personal
responsibility issue.

00:37:30.090 --> 00:37:30.917
All right.

00:37:30.917 --> 00:37:32.250
What lessons from skateboarding?

00:37:32.250 --> 00:37:34.110
The failure part.

00:37:34.110 --> 00:37:36.180
The failure,
failure, or failure.

00:37:36.180 --> 00:37:39.960
I mean, for me skateboarding--
never was a good skateboarder.

00:37:39.960 --> 00:37:42.090
I still have close
friends in that community

00:37:42.090 --> 00:37:44.160
and our photographer
and a guy who

00:37:44.160 --> 00:37:46.590
does all the visuals
and the other guys

00:37:46.590 --> 00:37:50.400
do the visuals for our podcast,
Mike Blabac, Chris, and Martin,

00:37:50.400 --> 00:37:52.200
all of that community.

00:37:52.200 --> 00:37:54.660
I think that for me that
community was really--

00:37:54.660 --> 00:37:58.830
as Mike will sometimes say,
skateboarders hate everything,

00:37:58.830 --> 00:38:00.930
meaning they have a very
high threshold for what

00:38:00.930 --> 00:38:02.137
they consider acceptable.

00:38:02.137 --> 00:38:03.220
It's not just what you do.

00:38:03.220 --> 00:38:04.350
It's how you do it.

00:38:04.350 --> 00:38:05.670
Super important.

00:38:05.670 --> 00:38:08.220
And I think in
neuroscience, there

00:38:08.220 --> 00:38:10.170
are a lot of-- there's
a lot of stuff.

00:38:10.170 --> 00:38:12.923
In science in general,
there are so many papers

00:38:12.923 --> 00:38:14.340
and there are so
many experiments.

00:38:14.340 --> 00:38:16.110
How do you navigate
that landscape?

00:38:16.110 --> 00:38:18.642
I think it helped me
develop a sense of taste.

00:38:18.642 --> 00:38:20.100
But the taste that
I'm referring to

00:38:20.100 --> 00:38:22.440
is not necessarily
a taste of which

00:38:22.440 --> 00:38:23.850
science is cool or not cool.

00:38:23.850 --> 00:38:26.580
That too, but it came
through a few times

00:38:26.580 --> 00:38:28.920
tonight when I was
talking about my mentors.

00:38:28.920 --> 00:38:31.890
I picked back then
skateboarding because I really

00:38:31.890 --> 00:38:33.030
liked the people.

00:38:33.030 --> 00:38:36.070
And also you didn't need
your parents to go to a game.

00:38:36.070 --> 00:38:38.160
And so that worked for me.

00:38:38.160 --> 00:38:40.740
And you could kind of
make your own schedule.

00:38:40.740 --> 00:38:43.830
And I do think it's very
important to the extent

00:38:43.830 --> 00:38:46.725
that you can, in science
and in everything,

00:38:46.725 --> 00:38:48.600
to surround yourself
with the kinds of people

00:38:48.600 --> 00:38:50.710
that you just really
enjoy being around.

00:38:50.710 --> 00:38:54.060
And so to me, the
podcast, running a lab,

00:38:54.060 --> 00:38:55.920
feels a lot like skateboarding.

00:38:55.920 --> 00:38:57.130
It's the same energy.

00:38:57.130 --> 00:39:00.810
It's the same neurochemical
systems firing, so that's--

00:39:00.810 --> 00:39:01.800
yeah, that one.

00:39:01.800 --> 00:39:04.650
Next?

00:39:04.650 --> 00:39:06.020
Favorite Feynman.

00:39:06.020 --> 00:39:08.580
Oh, I know that's inappropriate.

00:39:08.580 --> 00:39:12.780
I do have a Feynman story,
but it's inappropriate.

00:39:12.780 --> 00:39:13.520
Darn it.

00:39:13.520 --> 00:39:15.360
Damn.

00:39:15.360 --> 00:39:16.200
Maybe some time.

00:39:16.200 --> 00:39:18.055
This is why I don't drink.

00:39:18.055 --> 00:39:18.930
Good decision making.

00:39:21.920 --> 00:39:25.090
Well, I read all
of Feynman's books.

00:39:25.090 --> 00:39:27.710
So I had the pleasure
I never met him.

00:39:27.710 --> 00:39:30.970
He was dead before I was born.

00:39:30.970 --> 00:39:32.830
But my dad did.

00:39:32.830 --> 00:39:36.520
And he had good Feynman stories
and they were inappropriate.

00:39:36.520 --> 00:39:43.640
So the cool thing about Feynman
was that he didn't really

00:39:43.640 --> 00:39:48.230
care if people understood
the specifics of what

00:39:48.230 --> 00:39:49.590
he was talking about.

00:39:49.590 --> 00:39:51.290
He just wanted
people to get turned

00:39:51.290 --> 00:39:53.480
on to how amazing physics was.

00:39:53.480 --> 00:39:55.350
And he loved general principles.

00:39:55.350 --> 00:39:57.838
And one of the
things-- the example

00:39:57.838 --> 00:39:58.880
that sometimes given out.

00:39:58.880 --> 00:40:00.740
I don't know how many of you're
familiar with the Feynman

00:40:00.740 --> 00:40:01.040
books.

00:40:01.040 --> 00:40:02.607
But surely, you're
joking Mr Feynman

00:40:02.607 --> 00:40:04.940
or what do you care what other
people think, all of that

00:40:04.940 --> 00:40:06.110
stuff it's wonderful.

00:40:06.110 --> 00:40:07.190
He picked locks.

00:40:07.190 --> 00:40:08.730
When he worked at
Los Alamos Labs,

00:40:08.730 --> 00:40:09.980
they were working on the bomb.

00:40:09.980 --> 00:40:12.260
And he basically-- well,
there and elsewhere.

00:40:12.260 --> 00:40:14.630
And every morning the
offices used to come in

00:40:14.630 --> 00:40:17.565
and he would spread all the top
secret papers out on the floor.

00:40:17.565 --> 00:40:19.190
He would break into
the safes at night.

00:40:19.190 --> 00:40:20.720
And then they were
perplexed who could do this.

00:40:20.720 --> 00:40:22.010
And he liked safe cracking.

00:40:22.010 --> 00:40:25.040
Literally like national
security secrets just

00:40:25.040 --> 00:40:26.600
for fun, prankster.

00:40:26.600 --> 00:40:29.540
He also bongo drummed naked
on the roof of Caltech.

00:40:29.540 --> 00:40:32.805
And he did most of his writing
of theorems in strip clubs,

00:40:32.805 --> 00:40:33.305
in fact.

00:40:37.800 --> 00:40:39.650
Learnt to draw late
in life, was really

00:40:39.650 --> 00:40:44.210
into flotation tanks,
and very curious about

00:40:44.210 --> 00:40:46.010
but never did psychedelics.

00:40:46.010 --> 00:40:48.000
That's as I understand.

00:40:48.000 --> 00:40:51.020
But one of the cool
Feynman factoids

00:40:51.020 --> 00:40:54.140
is that when he
was a kid, he talks

00:40:54.140 --> 00:40:56.060
about when he was a
child that his dad used

00:40:56.060 --> 00:40:57.470
to take him birdwatching.

00:40:57.470 --> 00:41:00.440
And he'd say, oh, well
that's a whatever scrub jay

00:41:00.440 --> 00:41:04.470
and that's whatever thrush and
that's the-- and his dad said,

00:41:04.470 --> 00:41:11.710
no don't cloud your mind
with naming and taxonomy.

00:41:11.710 --> 00:41:13.300
That's not meaningful.

00:41:13.300 --> 00:41:14.980
Because then what
if it's different?

00:41:14.980 --> 00:41:19.030
The pigmy thrush or the
lesser or this or that.

00:41:19.030 --> 00:41:20.530
The more important
thing is to start

00:41:20.530 --> 00:41:25.492
to identify principles of
why certain birds behave

00:41:25.492 --> 00:41:27.200
one way and certain
birds behave another.

00:41:27.200 --> 00:41:29.900
And to start finding
the commonalities

00:41:29.900 --> 00:41:31.310
and the regularities.

00:41:31.310 --> 00:41:33.470
And that's a theme that
I obviously tonight

00:41:33.470 --> 00:41:35.330
have tried to impose.

00:41:35.330 --> 00:41:37.880
And it's actually something
that I can't do in podcasts,

00:41:37.880 --> 00:41:40.437
necessarily because I can't
thread across 40 episodes.

00:41:40.437 --> 00:41:42.020
Or something like
that in the same way

00:41:42.020 --> 00:41:43.640
that I could in an
evening like this.

00:41:43.640 --> 00:41:47.630
So that's an appropriate
Feynman story.

00:41:47.630 --> 00:41:50.043
Also he just seemed
like a delightful guy.

00:41:50.043 --> 00:41:50.960
And he's kind of cool.

00:41:50.960 --> 00:41:53.188
He's a little bit street,
he had the thick accent.

00:41:53.188 --> 00:41:55.730
He was from Far Rockaway, but
he didn't really care much what

00:41:55.730 --> 00:41:58.363
people thought, or he did
and he pretended he didn't.

00:41:58.363 --> 00:41:59.780
Care for when
people tell you they

00:41:59.780 --> 00:42:01.110
don't care what people think.

00:42:01.110 --> 00:42:05.330
I think he did to the extent
that it still allowed him

00:42:05.330 --> 00:42:07.580
to get the message out there.

00:42:07.580 --> 00:42:11.110
OK, next question please.

00:42:11.110 --> 00:42:13.320
My horse.

00:42:13.320 --> 00:42:13.820
Wow.

00:42:16.890 --> 00:42:17.520
I love this.

00:42:17.520 --> 00:42:20.283
I delight in all things
animals, but especially horses,

00:42:20.283 --> 00:42:22.200
because my high school
girlfriend had a horse.

00:42:22.200 --> 00:42:27.600
And they do that thing where
people go, oh you know, horses

00:42:27.600 --> 00:42:30.345
can detect how-- they know
more about you than you know.

00:42:30.345 --> 00:42:33.000
And then I get onto the horse,
and the horse like this this

00:42:33.000 --> 00:42:33.500
and this.

00:42:33.500 --> 00:42:35.040
And it's like a litmus test.

00:42:35.040 --> 00:42:37.740
Having a girlfriend with a horse
was very intimidating for me

00:42:37.740 --> 00:42:38.520
actually.

00:42:38.520 --> 00:42:41.010
I felt like I had to
compete with the horse.

00:42:41.010 --> 00:42:43.485
She spent all this time with
the horse, it's very large.

00:42:49.630 --> 00:42:53.590
Anyway, eventually
I broke the horse.

00:42:53.590 --> 00:42:57.130
OK, my horse does the double
inhale, long exhale often.

00:42:57.130 --> 00:42:58.390
He's a bit of a stressed guy.

00:42:58.390 --> 00:42:59.290
Warm blood?

00:42:59.290 --> 00:43:01.240
Yeah, warm blood.

00:43:01.240 --> 00:43:04.930
I used to work at the barn.

00:43:04.930 --> 00:43:06.812
I used to shovel manure
and work at the barn.

00:43:06.812 --> 00:43:08.270
She brought her
horse to college. .

00:43:08.270 --> 00:43:09.800
I actually followed
her off to college

00:43:09.800 --> 00:43:11.217
I never would have
gone to college

00:43:11.217 --> 00:43:13.360
if she hadn't gone to college.

00:43:13.360 --> 00:43:16.600
And the horses are
interesting animals.

00:43:16.600 --> 00:43:18.393
They do tell you a lot.

00:43:18.393 --> 00:43:20.560
The horse does the double
inhale, long exhale often.

00:43:20.560 --> 00:43:21.730
He's a bit of a stress guy.

00:43:21.730 --> 00:43:23.620
Do you suppose this
physiological stress regulator

00:43:23.620 --> 00:43:24.412
transcends species?

00:43:24.412 --> 00:43:26.770
Absolutely.

00:43:26.770 --> 00:43:29.140
In fact, I mentioned warm blood.

00:43:29.140 --> 00:43:33.230
I have a colleague at
Stanford and she's amazing.

00:43:33.230 --> 00:43:35.290
Her name is Sue
McConnell and she

00:43:35.290 --> 00:43:37.540
is an expert in dog
genetics, so you

00:43:37.540 --> 00:43:41.530
can imagine I'm always
asking her questions.

00:43:41.530 --> 00:43:45.110
And we talk about dogs and we
talk about horses because she

00:43:45.110 --> 00:43:45.610
also--

00:43:45.610 --> 00:43:48.460
I thinks she raises warm bloods.

00:43:48.460 --> 00:43:51.220
And you hear about hot
bloods and warm bloods.

00:43:51.220 --> 00:43:54.400
And also, if you have any
familiarity with dogs,

00:43:54.400 --> 00:43:57.370
there are dogs like Costello,
or like a nuclear bomb

00:43:57.370 --> 00:44:00.810
could go off and Costello
might open an eye.

00:44:00.810 --> 00:44:03.930
That's the bulldog,
economy of effort.

00:44:03.930 --> 00:44:06.330
They're not going to get
activated unless there's

00:44:06.330 --> 00:44:07.080
a reason to do it.

00:44:07.080 --> 00:44:10.650
They are very, as we call
it, parasympathetic dominant.

00:44:10.650 --> 00:44:15.930
That seesaw of autonomic
arousal is just really relaxed.

00:44:15.930 --> 00:44:19.990
Getting them into action
is more of an effort.

00:44:19.990 --> 00:44:25.140
There are other animals
like the whippet

00:44:25.140 --> 00:44:27.600
or the Italian greyhound.

00:44:27.600 --> 00:44:33.030
Like they're always cold, that
are very sympathetic dominant.

00:44:33.030 --> 00:44:35.490
And then of course, within
a breed or within a species

00:44:35.490 --> 00:44:36.690
there's a range.

00:44:36.690 --> 00:44:40.470
And humans also,
are within a range.

00:44:40.470 --> 00:44:43.770
I think anyone who's had
children will tell you he

00:44:43.770 --> 00:44:45.750
or she has been like
this since birth.

00:44:45.750 --> 00:44:49.680
Calm, easygoing, or like
really easily stressed.

00:44:49.680 --> 00:44:53.250
I think that seesaw, we didn't
get into tonight too much.

00:44:53.250 --> 00:44:56.550
But there's a concept with
the autonomic regulation

00:44:56.550 --> 00:44:57.587
of a hinge.

00:44:57.587 --> 00:44:59.670
So don't think so much
about being really stressed

00:44:59.670 --> 00:45:00.600
out or really relaxed.

00:45:00.600 --> 00:45:02.520
But certain animals,
the hinge is

00:45:02.520 --> 00:45:05.700
tighten so that the
seesaw just kind of tilts

00:45:05.700 --> 00:45:07.530
mellow like Costello.

00:45:07.530 --> 00:45:11.400
A bulldog almost seems
like a different animal

00:45:11.400 --> 00:45:13.050
than a whippet.

00:45:13.050 --> 00:45:14.700
They're so very different.

00:45:14.700 --> 00:45:16.950
And within the
category of horses--

00:45:16.950 --> 00:45:19.920
and I'm not an expert
in horse genetics,

00:45:19.920 --> 00:45:22.290
but they are
selected for not just

00:45:22.290 --> 00:45:28.530
for their physical attributes,
but for their psychological or

00:45:28.530 --> 00:45:30.330
temperament attributes.

00:45:30.330 --> 00:45:32.140
And you see this in dogs too.

00:45:32.140 --> 00:45:34.050
In fact, the reason
I picked Costello,

00:45:34.050 --> 00:45:36.150
and Elvis can verify
the stories, I read--

00:45:36.150 --> 00:45:38.760
I wanted a dog
for so many years.

00:45:38.760 --> 00:45:42.480
And I went there and there
were all these puppies.

00:45:42.480 --> 00:45:46.350
And I was like I heard you need
to take them in the other room

00:45:46.350 --> 00:45:47.015
one by one.

00:45:47.015 --> 00:45:49.140
And then if it barks for
its siblings, you're like,

00:45:49.140 --> 00:45:50.580
oh it's a healthy puppy.

00:45:50.580 --> 00:45:53.190
So I walk in and all the dogs
are running around like crazy.

00:45:53.190 --> 00:45:55.190
It was right around
Christmas time, right Elvis?

00:45:55.190 --> 00:45:58.020
And they're running around and
then there's one in the back.

00:45:58.020 --> 00:45:59.670
And he's taking
advantage of the fact

00:45:59.670 --> 00:46:01.170
that all the other
ones are running.

00:46:01.170 --> 00:46:03.510
He's just eating out
of all of their bowls.

00:46:03.510 --> 00:46:05.650
And I was like, I want that one.

00:46:05.650 --> 00:46:08.580
So I took that chubby little
bastard in the next room

00:46:08.580 --> 00:46:12.720
and I thought, OK, he's going
to bark for his siblings.

00:46:12.720 --> 00:46:14.730
And he lay down
and he took a nap.

00:46:14.730 --> 00:46:15.960
And I was like, this one.

00:46:15.960 --> 00:46:17.310
I want this one.

00:46:17.310 --> 00:46:18.840
Why did I want that one?

00:46:18.840 --> 00:46:21.870
Well, this completes
the principle

00:46:21.870 --> 00:46:26.150
which is, I wanted a dog like
that because I'm not like that.

00:46:26.150 --> 00:46:29.030
And I was very interested in
a dog I could take care of,

00:46:29.030 --> 00:46:32.550
but also a dog that would help
regulate my nervous system.

00:46:32.550 --> 00:46:34.280
And so for me having
a dog like that

00:46:34.280 --> 00:46:35.930
as opposed to a
whippet or something

00:46:35.930 --> 00:46:39.770
that was going constantly
around is a very calming effect.

00:46:39.770 --> 00:46:43.050
And to this day memory of his
snoring still puts me to sleep.

00:46:43.050 --> 00:46:45.668
So I think that your
horse probably has--

00:46:45.668 --> 00:46:47.210
it kind of idles a
little bit higher.

00:46:47.210 --> 00:46:49.010
Think about the rpm.

00:46:49.010 --> 00:46:51.380
You know, revs a little bit
higher at a given speed--

00:46:51.380 --> 00:46:53.017
more rpm at a given speed.

00:46:53.017 --> 00:46:55.100
That's the way I think
about the autonomic system.

00:46:55.100 --> 00:46:56.750
How do you reset that?

00:46:56.750 --> 00:47:00.260
Well, this is why a lot of
exercise is good, right?

00:47:00.260 --> 00:47:02.910
And certainly my
girlfriend's horse was crazy.

00:47:02.910 --> 00:47:04.220
It was gelded late.

00:47:04.220 --> 00:47:06.600
And it was crazy.

00:47:06.600 --> 00:47:09.230
I almost said nuts but
that's like a bad pun

00:47:09.230 --> 00:47:12.720
so it was not nuts
but it was crazy.

00:47:12.720 --> 00:47:13.625
It was gelded late.

00:47:16.420 --> 00:47:17.065
Next question.

00:47:20.760 --> 00:47:24.240
Is there any science behind
staying motivated or developing

00:47:24.240 --> 00:47:25.030
discipline?

00:47:25.030 --> 00:47:27.875
Oh, so this represents
the higher tier

00:47:27.875 --> 00:47:29.250
of where I think
things are going

00:47:29.250 --> 00:47:30.667
to go in the next
few years, where

00:47:30.667 --> 00:47:33.900
we're going to start seeing
this convergence of psychology

00:47:33.900 --> 00:47:38.970
and biology, where we can
get to these harder concepts.

00:47:38.970 --> 00:47:41.700
I like to think that
we can stay motivated

00:47:41.700 --> 00:47:45.380
through a simple process that
now will make sense to you.

00:47:45.380 --> 00:47:48.560
Because the last thing I covered
was toggling back and forth

00:47:48.560 --> 00:47:52.430
between our ability to
be gritty and linen,

00:47:52.430 --> 00:47:55.100
kind of in friction, maybe
even a little anger, fear,

00:47:55.100 --> 00:47:56.450
competitiveness, et cetera.

00:47:56.450 --> 00:47:58.670
That kind of grinding in.

00:47:58.670 --> 00:48:02.520
But that the more
sustaining fuel,

00:48:02.520 --> 00:48:08.520
the sort of hybrid version,
a hybrid fuel model

00:48:08.520 --> 00:48:10.560
would be one in which
you can access that.

00:48:10.560 --> 00:48:14.770
But that's a depletable
and not so renewable

00:48:14.770 --> 00:48:16.420
resource without a lot of rest.

00:48:16.420 --> 00:48:19.660
Meaning, working hard out of
anger, determination, and kind

00:48:19.660 --> 00:48:23.980
of grit will work, but
when you are depleted

00:48:23.980 --> 00:48:26.410
you have to stop
for a long while.

00:48:26.410 --> 00:48:30.110
Whereas if you can access
this delight system,

00:48:30.110 --> 00:48:34.390
which is really one of
dopamine and serotonin, both.

00:48:34.390 --> 00:48:37.840
In other words, and I want
to think of a different way

00:48:37.840 --> 00:48:40.030
to put this, but to try
and think about what

00:48:40.030 --> 00:48:46.240
sorts of things and tools allow
you to be and feel most loving,

00:48:46.240 --> 00:48:49.000
I know it sounds weak.

00:48:49.000 --> 00:48:50.770
But it's anything
but weak to be most

00:48:50.770 --> 00:48:54.550
loving in the verb sense of the
word toward what you're doing.

00:48:54.550 --> 00:48:56.620
I actually used to use
this trick in college when

00:48:56.620 --> 00:48:58.990
I'd encounter a topic I hated.

00:48:58.990 --> 00:49:02.110
I would tell myself I'm really--

00:49:02.110 --> 00:49:04.240
I'm just going to
fall in love with this

00:49:04.240 --> 00:49:06.250
by trying to find
the gems within it.

00:49:06.250 --> 00:49:07.090
Sometimes it worked.

00:49:07.090 --> 00:49:07.923
Sometimes it didn't.

00:49:07.923 --> 00:49:11.080
But the wish to do it
that way as opposed to OK,

00:49:11.080 --> 00:49:13.100
I'm just going to
grind this out,

00:49:13.100 --> 00:49:16.280
at least for me at the
time was a powerful tool.

00:49:16.280 --> 00:49:19.690
So motivation and--
discipline is a tricky one.

00:49:19.690 --> 00:49:22.630
That's sort of the
just do it thing.

00:49:22.630 --> 00:49:26.710
You need tools to modulate your
stress and to get your sleep--

00:49:26.710 --> 00:49:28.180
do all the basic things right.

00:49:28.180 --> 00:49:31.850
Set the right context for
you to be in your best

00:49:31.850 --> 00:49:34.610
chance of being disciplined.

00:49:34.610 --> 00:49:37.560
And that itself is its
own form of discipline.

00:49:37.560 --> 00:49:41.390
But in terms of
continual motivation

00:49:41.390 --> 00:49:45.880
you're not going to manage to
go against the grain for very

00:49:45.880 --> 00:49:47.770
long.

00:49:47.770 --> 00:49:50.110
People have managed to
go against challenge

00:49:50.110 --> 00:49:51.550
for a very long time--

00:49:51.550 --> 00:49:52.970
for a very long times.

00:49:52.970 --> 00:49:55.600
In fact, I was reading recently
about the psychology of people

00:49:55.600 --> 00:49:57.100
who've been kidnapped.

00:49:57.100 --> 00:49:59.415
And they have this odd
trick that they used.

00:49:59.415 --> 00:50:01.540
Have you heard about this
is sort of like Stockholm

00:50:01.540 --> 00:50:02.040
syndrome?

00:50:02.040 --> 00:50:04.630
But they actually convinced
themselves to fall in love

00:50:04.630 --> 00:50:05.920
with their captors.

00:50:05.920 --> 00:50:08.750
And then they come up with
new ways to escape them,

00:50:08.750 --> 00:50:10.910
which is kind of cool.

00:50:10.910 --> 00:50:14.170
So there's something
about mentally feeling

00:50:14.170 --> 00:50:16.180
like you're trying to go
from back on your heels

00:50:16.180 --> 00:50:17.890
to flat footed.

00:50:17.890 --> 00:50:20.630
That's very
energetically costly.

00:50:20.630 --> 00:50:26.620
So again, these systems are
very susceptible to what we call

00:50:26.620 --> 00:50:28.205
context or top down regulation.

00:50:28.205 --> 00:50:29.080
Hopefully that helps.

00:50:29.080 --> 00:50:30.640
I know it's a
little bit abstract.

00:50:30.640 --> 00:50:32.650
I wish I could give
you a 1-minute exercise

00:50:32.650 --> 00:50:34.330
or make you motivated.

00:50:34.330 --> 00:50:37.600
But we do talk about tools to
get adrenaline going and things

00:50:37.600 --> 00:50:38.170
like that.

00:50:38.170 --> 00:50:40.630
But spend some time
thinking about what

00:50:40.630 --> 00:50:42.310
would allow you
to sustain effort

00:50:42.310 --> 00:50:43.970
through positive feelings.

00:50:43.970 --> 00:50:47.870
It's not a light concept at all.

00:50:47.870 --> 00:50:48.900
Next question please.

00:50:48.900 --> 00:50:50.650
What would be your
biggest piece of advice

00:50:50.650 --> 00:50:52.060
for achieving one's dreams?

00:50:52.060 --> 00:50:52.930
Boy!

00:50:52.930 --> 00:50:54.160
That's a tough one.

00:50:59.425 --> 00:51:01.300
Again, this is going to
be a little abstract.

00:51:06.950 --> 00:51:13.670
I'm a believer in this idea
kind of a seed message.

00:51:13.670 --> 00:51:17.030
Robert Greene has
talked a lot about this,

00:51:17.030 --> 00:51:21.650
that we can all kind of
think back to a event

00:51:21.650 --> 00:51:22.850
or stage of our life.

00:51:22.850 --> 00:51:27.140
Typically it's before
puberty for other reasons

00:51:27.140 --> 00:51:28.490
that are kind of interesting.

00:51:28.490 --> 00:51:31.260
But where we delight
in something.

00:51:31.260 --> 00:51:32.390
So for me it was fish.

00:51:32.390 --> 00:51:34.910
And obviously now I don't
need to work on fish.

00:51:34.910 --> 00:51:36.470
It wasn't about the fish.

00:51:36.470 --> 00:51:37.640
I hope that came through.

00:51:37.640 --> 00:51:42.010
I mean aquaria are really cool,
but it's not about the fish.

00:51:42.010 --> 00:51:43.760
It was something about
the way they moved.

00:51:43.760 --> 00:51:46.520
It was something about the way
that it tickled my excitement.

00:51:46.520 --> 00:51:48.562
I used to get dropped off
at this little pet shop

00:51:48.562 --> 00:51:50.840
in California Avenue
called Monette's Pet Shop.

00:51:50.840 --> 00:51:52.400
And my mom used it as childcare.

00:51:52.400 --> 00:51:54.358
She would drop me off
there and I had this book

00:51:54.358 --> 00:51:56.180
and I would log all
the tropical fish,

00:51:56.180 --> 00:51:58.820
and which ones could be with
which ones, and then I would--

00:51:58.820 --> 00:52:00.170
I was obsessed.

00:52:00.170 --> 00:52:02.090
But for me it was
something about organizing

00:52:02.090 --> 00:52:04.490
and being able to make
reliable predictions.

00:52:04.490 --> 00:52:05.600
It was about parsimony.

00:52:05.600 --> 00:52:08.660
It was about principles
as opposed to--

00:52:08.660 --> 00:52:11.390
and the colors delighted me
and all that kind of stuff.

00:52:11.390 --> 00:52:14.060
The equipment delighted
me, but then I hit puberty.

00:52:14.060 --> 00:52:17.300
And then it was something else.

00:52:17.300 --> 00:52:20.000
And then I went to college
and it was something else.

00:52:20.000 --> 00:52:22.500
And I got a girlfriend
and it was something else.

00:52:22.500 --> 00:52:25.970
So it changes over time.

00:52:25.970 --> 00:52:29.900
But this is why I recommend to
that young 15-year-old person

00:52:29.900 --> 00:52:34.340
that they learn to tap into that
sense of like Oh, this is cool.

00:52:34.340 --> 00:52:35.300
Like this feels cool.

00:52:35.300 --> 00:52:37.310
I know not everyone
else thinks it's cool.

00:52:37.310 --> 00:52:38.030
Maybe they do.

00:52:38.030 --> 00:52:39.930
Like this feels good.

00:52:39.930 --> 00:52:41.960
I actually have a somatic
experience of this.

00:52:41.960 --> 00:52:43.890
I'm not a very semantically
oriented person.

00:52:43.890 --> 00:52:44.720
I'm more up here.

00:52:44.720 --> 00:52:48.320
But I actually know if
I'm on to something,

00:52:48.320 --> 00:52:50.510
if this left arm just
kind of starts fidgeting,

00:52:50.510 --> 00:52:52.550
it's like I want to move
or like some people--

00:52:52.550 --> 00:52:55.940
you can start to identify
ways in which you suddenly

00:52:55.940 --> 00:52:57.660
have this positive energy.

00:52:57.660 --> 00:52:59.100
It's not a fear energy.

00:52:59.100 --> 00:53:01.370
It's almost like a
magnetism to things.

00:53:01.370 --> 00:53:05.090
And just don't be
confused or misdirected

00:53:05.090 --> 00:53:06.980
in thinking that
it's that thing.

00:53:06.980 --> 00:53:11.420
It's that energy or that
attraction to something

00:53:11.420 --> 00:53:15.200
that feels right that is your--

00:53:15.200 --> 00:53:18.050
I wish we had these
divining rod to find water.

00:53:18.050 --> 00:53:19.370
That's your tool.

00:53:19.370 --> 00:53:22.620
It's like antennae, you
want to grow your antennae.

00:53:22.620 --> 00:53:24.950
So how do you
follow your dreams?

00:53:24.950 --> 00:53:28.290
Well, I never thought
I'd do a podcast.

00:53:28.290 --> 00:53:30.140
I never thought I'd
become a neuroscientist.

00:53:30.140 --> 00:53:32.600
You have to be willing
of course, to take risks

00:53:32.600 --> 00:53:37.070
and to iterate quickly,
but not so quickly

00:53:37.070 --> 00:53:39.980
that you fail out of
the game, et cetera,

00:53:39.980 --> 00:53:41.630
if you do get back
in, et cetera.

00:53:41.630 --> 00:53:44.245
But it's really about
developing an awareness.

00:53:44.245 --> 00:53:46.370
Now, the key thing is you're
not going to find this

00:53:46.370 --> 00:53:49.070
by going up a mountain and
sitting there or waiting

00:53:49.070 --> 00:53:51.620
for your passion to
just kind of rock it in,

00:53:51.620 --> 00:53:52.843
or piano fall onto your head.

00:53:52.843 --> 00:53:54.260
It's not going to
happen that way.

00:53:54.260 --> 00:53:57.020
You have to interact
with the sensory world

00:53:57.020 --> 00:53:58.550
and different kinds of people.

00:53:58.550 --> 00:54:03.050
And you have to be a little bit
of a adventurer in a safe way,

00:54:03.050 --> 00:54:04.310
of course.

00:54:04.310 --> 00:54:08.600
An adventure and learn
to recognize the signals.

00:54:08.600 --> 00:54:12.020
And some people are
very in tune with this.

00:54:12.020 --> 00:54:14.660
There's an amazing podcast
with Rick Rubin recently

00:54:14.660 --> 00:54:16.790
on Joe Rogan's
Podcast where he talks

00:54:16.790 --> 00:54:18.350
about the creative processes.

00:54:18.350 --> 00:54:21.350
Kind of this-- it
seems like whatever's

00:54:21.350 --> 00:54:23.730
going on in that beard of his
just connects to the world.

00:54:23.730 --> 00:54:25.640
And he can just like--

00:54:25.640 --> 00:54:28.580
there, that's where you
need to go and that's--

00:54:28.580 --> 00:54:32.570
But that's part of the magic,
is you don't really know.

00:54:32.570 --> 00:54:35.300
And because it's all energetic--

00:54:35.300 --> 00:54:36.890
it's all energetic.

00:54:36.890 --> 00:54:38.420
And when I say
energetic, I don't

00:54:38.420 --> 00:54:39.320
mean in the mystical sense.

00:54:39.320 --> 00:54:40.695
I mean you have
to learn to sense

00:54:40.695 --> 00:54:42.020
those fluctuations in energy.

00:54:42.020 --> 00:54:43.853
Some people can sense
them very easily

00:54:43.853 --> 00:54:45.020
because they're very mellow.

00:54:45.020 --> 00:54:46.728
And if something gets
them really excited

00:54:46.728 --> 00:54:50.600
they notice as a big delta, as
we say in science, big change.

00:54:50.600 --> 00:54:53.940
Other people they ride
kind of high all the time.

00:54:53.940 --> 00:54:55.910
And so everything
is exciting to them,

00:54:55.910 --> 00:54:58.280
and they miss a lot of
the subtle fluctuations

00:54:58.280 --> 00:55:00.210
in what's really special
and right for them.

00:55:00.210 --> 00:55:03.470
In fact, mania is
characterized by hyper

00:55:03.470 --> 00:55:08.200
elevated levels of dopamine
and everything is a good idea.

00:55:08.200 --> 00:55:09.730
And depression is the opposite.

00:55:09.730 --> 00:55:11.170
Nothing is a good idea.

00:55:11.170 --> 00:55:13.120
Nothing's going to work.

00:55:13.120 --> 00:55:14.470
And those are the extremes.

00:55:14.470 --> 00:55:17.770
And those are rough
conditions obviously.

00:55:17.770 --> 00:55:20.080
But for most people,
it's about learning

00:55:20.080 --> 00:55:21.970
to detect those
subtle fluctuations.

00:55:21.970 --> 00:55:25.900
And every time-- every single
time you find somebody who is

00:55:25.900 --> 00:55:28.068
exceptional at their craft
and doing well in life--

00:55:28.068 --> 00:55:30.610
there are a lot of people who
are exceptional at their craft,

00:55:30.610 --> 00:55:34.650
but not necessarily
doing well on the whole.

00:55:34.650 --> 00:55:38.850
Those people have a
kind of intuition about

00:55:38.850 --> 00:55:39.998
what feels good to them.

00:55:39.998 --> 00:55:41.790
This year's Nobel Prize
winner in chemistry

00:55:41.790 --> 00:55:43.740
is my colleague,
Carolyn Bertozzi.

00:55:43.740 --> 00:55:46.680
And all I know of
her, except the fact

00:55:46.680 --> 00:55:49.123
that she's an amazing
chemist, is they

00:55:49.123 --> 00:55:50.290
did this interview with her.

00:55:50.290 --> 00:55:52.200
And she said that when everyone
would go out in college

00:55:52.200 --> 00:55:54.090
she was finding excuses
to stay home and read

00:55:54.090 --> 00:55:55.200
organic chemistry.

00:55:55.200 --> 00:55:58.700
Now, that to me sounds
like a bad night.

00:55:58.700 --> 00:56:01.610
But for her it was pure delight.

00:56:01.610 --> 00:56:03.870
And she's wired for that.

00:56:03.870 --> 00:56:06.320
And I think her
work is going to be

00:56:06.320 --> 00:56:09.050
vitally important and
transformative for humanity.

00:56:09.050 --> 00:56:09.830
I really do.

00:56:09.830 --> 00:56:14.420
So how do you succeed
in chasing your dreams?

00:56:14.420 --> 00:56:16.200
You succeed in
identifying what they are.

00:56:16.200 --> 00:56:17.575
But you don't know
at the outset.

00:56:17.575 --> 00:56:23.780
You want to find the energy
to find the right path

00:56:23.780 --> 00:56:26.780
and continually course correct
when you will undoubtedly

00:56:26.780 --> 00:56:28.453
be off your path.

00:56:28.453 --> 00:56:29.870
That's essentially
what I've done.

00:56:29.870 --> 00:56:32.390
I still look for the
feeling of delighting

00:56:32.390 --> 00:56:34.280
in Costello or the cuttlefish.

00:56:34.280 --> 00:56:35.570
That's what I'm looking for.

00:56:35.570 --> 00:56:38.275
It's not a template I have to
match, but that's my like, oh,

00:56:38.275 --> 00:56:39.650
yeah I know what
that feels like.

00:56:39.650 --> 00:56:40.773
It's like a texture.

00:56:40.773 --> 00:56:43.190
It's like if you think about
a bunch of different textures

00:56:43.190 --> 00:56:46.730
of sandpaper, it's like this
one that just feels really good.

00:56:46.730 --> 00:56:49.730
And so you're comparing
everything to that,

00:56:49.730 --> 00:56:52.970
because the system that
involves all these chemicals,

00:56:52.970 --> 00:56:55.640
you'll find it if you learn
to pay attention to it.

00:56:55.640 --> 00:56:58.670
But you won't find it sitting,
staring at your belly button

00:56:58.670 --> 00:57:00.120
or going up a mountain.

00:57:00.120 --> 00:57:04.430
You have to be in sensory
experience in order to find it.

00:57:04.430 --> 00:57:08.850
Reflection is good but you
need to get into action.

00:57:08.850 --> 00:57:09.350
Wow.

00:57:09.350 --> 00:57:11.330
All right well.

00:57:11.330 --> 00:57:13.430
OK, so psilocybin.

00:57:13.430 --> 00:57:14.600
Opinion of the psychedelics.

00:57:14.600 --> 00:57:16.790
Generally, we just had an
episode with my colleague Nolan

00:57:16.790 --> 00:57:19.010
Williams, who's a triple
board certified neurologist

00:57:19.010 --> 00:57:19.880
psychiatrist.

00:57:19.880 --> 00:57:21.290
It's a fun thing about
working at Stanford.

00:57:21.290 --> 00:57:23.123
It's also very humbling
because you're like,

00:57:23.123 --> 00:57:25.136
whoa who are these people?

00:57:25.136 --> 00:57:26.930
They have three
board certifications.

00:57:26.930 --> 00:57:30.060
The psilocybin-- first of
all, not for everybody.

00:57:30.060 --> 00:57:32.690
People with psychosis,
it is still illegal

00:57:32.690 --> 00:57:35.590
decriminalized in
certain places.

00:57:35.590 --> 00:57:38.050
So obviously the
cautionary notes

00:57:38.050 --> 00:57:41.770
people who have drug addiction
issues or other kinds

00:57:41.770 --> 00:57:44.620
of addiction issues need to
be thoughtful about diving

00:57:44.620 --> 00:57:46.450
into a neurochemical
landscape like that.

00:57:46.450 --> 00:57:51.250
But it does appear that
the clinical trials

00:57:51.250 --> 00:57:52.965
on one macro dose.

00:57:52.965 --> 00:57:54.340
This is what's
interesting to me.

00:57:54.340 --> 00:57:56.590
A lot of people talk about
micro-dosing psilocybin,

00:57:56.590 --> 00:58:00.010
but the data at least according
to Matthew Johnson, who is also

00:58:00.010 --> 00:58:02.860
on the podcast, the
data for micro-dosing

00:58:02.860 --> 00:58:05.410
are not really there, frankly.

00:58:05.410 --> 00:58:10.840
The data on single session macro
dose, the sort of heroic doses

00:58:10.840 --> 00:58:13.900
that have been talked about
in the psychonaut community

00:58:13.900 --> 00:58:17.980
for a depression and
to some extent PTSD,

00:58:17.980 --> 00:58:22.930
and for eating disorders and
sort of end of life preparation

00:58:22.930 --> 00:58:24.310
are quite encouraging.

00:58:24.310 --> 00:58:27.520
In fact, the current data
suggests that about 2/3

00:58:27.520 --> 00:58:30.550
of people achieve lasting
relief from one session.

00:58:30.550 --> 00:58:35.810
Now, keep in mind those are
guided sessions with physicians

00:58:35.810 --> 00:58:37.040
in the room, et cetera.

00:58:37.040 --> 00:58:39.770
I do think there's a potential
hazard of all psychedelics,

00:58:39.770 --> 00:58:41.990
which is they alter--

00:58:41.990 --> 00:58:44.360
this includes MDMA
or especially MDMA,

00:58:44.360 --> 00:58:46.610
they alter the chemical
landscape in you,

00:58:46.610 --> 00:58:49.310
such that a lot of
things can serve

00:58:49.310 --> 00:58:51.090
as attractors in that state.

00:58:51.090 --> 00:58:54.230
Meaning you can get really into
the sound of music in an MDMA

00:58:54.230 --> 00:58:57.470
session, feel connected
to that, and waste

00:58:57.470 --> 00:59:00.650
the opportunity for some more
meaningful transformative

00:59:00.650 --> 00:59:02.270
rewiring.

00:59:02.270 --> 00:59:06.000
And I do think that's
worth paying attention to.

00:59:06.000 --> 00:59:09.710
So that's the usefulness of
having a therapeutic guide,

00:59:09.710 --> 00:59:12.740
as they can continually
steer you back to what

00:59:12.740 --> 00:59:16.010
at least for you is the
more meaningful work.

00:59:16.010 --> 00:59:17.540
But it's very encouraging.

00:59:17.540 --> 00:59:22.010
And Nolan Williams,
who I trust, is again

00:59:22.010 --> 00:59:26.960
triple board certified M.D. Said
that in the studies of lifetime

00:59:26.960 --> 00:59:30.230
perceived individual and
societal risk, of all

00:59:30.230 --> 00:59:32.510
the compounds out there
except for caffeine,

00:59:32.510 --> 00:59:35.360
psilocybin is at the
bottom of the list.

00:59:35.360 --> 00:59:39.500
Whereas things like
heroin, cocaine, alcohol,

00:59:39.500 --> 00:59:41.750
methamphetamine sit at
the top of the list.

00:59:41.750 --> 00:59:45.260
Actually, alcohol quite high
on that list at certain amounts

00:59:45.260 --> 00:59:46.170
of consumption.

00:59:46.170 --> 00:59:49.610
So I'm very excited
about what's happening

00:59:49.610 --> 00:59:51.470
in the landscape of
psilocybin, but I'm not

00:59:51.470 --> 00:59:53.930
so excited about the
micro-dosing data.

00:59:53.930 --> 00:59:57.200
Very excited about the
single heroic dose data.

00:59:57.200 --> 00:59:59.450
One interesting
thing there perhaps,

00:59:59.450 --> 01:00:01.220
what seems to be
the unifying feature

01:00:01.220 --> 01:00:05.810
of a successful psilocybo--
psilocybin, excuse me session,

01:00:05.810 --> 01:00:08.630
is that at some point
the person feels

01:00:08.630 --> 01:00:12.590
as if it's like too much
of a autonomic thing.

01:00:12.590 --> 01:00:14.960
It kind of get to
this point and then

01:00:14.960 --> 01:00:16.910
they are encouraged to "let go."

01:00:16.910 --> 01:00:18.350
And I'm fascinated
by this concept

01:00:18.350 --> 01:00:20.630
of letting go, because
I'm a neuroscientist.

01:00:20.630 --> 01:00:22.910
We don't know what
that means, but it

01:00:22.910 --> 01:00:28.040
seems like being able to ride
the wave of autonomic arousal

01:00:28.040 --> 01:00:29.210
from top to bottom.

01:00:29.210 --> 01:00:31.430
Seems to be very powerful
for trauma and depression

01:00:31.430 --> 01:00:31.830
treatment.

01:00:31.830 --> 01:00:32.830
And this is interesting.

01:00:32.830 --> 01:00:36.200
A lot of people think that one
of the major issues in humans

01:00:36.200 --> 01:00:38.270
nowadays is we're stressed
about a lot of things.

01:00:38.270 --> 01:00:41.970
But we never actually get to go
into the full stress response,

01:00:41.970 --> 01:00:44.900
and then let it relax again.

01:00:44.900 --> 01:00:48.440
And catharsis was
big at one point.

01:00:48.440 --> 01:00:51.080
Scream therapy, Steve Jobs
is going to scream therapies.

01:00:51.080 --> 01:00:54.470
That whether or not catharsis is
healthy or not has been debated

01:00:54.470 --> 01:00:58.280
but the data are kind
of pointing to the fact

01:00:58.280 --> 01:01:01.460
that it may be provided
that the catharsis is not.

01:01:01.460 --> 01:01:04.430
Obviously someone damaging
themselves for somebody else.

01:01:04.430 --> 01:01:07.430
So maybe we should all
be screaming a lot more.

01:01:07.430 --> 01:01:09.830
Why does my desire
to eat disappear

01:01:09.830 --> 01:01:11.810
after I use the sauna?

01:01:11.810 --> 01:01:12.500
Oh, interesting.

01:01:12.500 --> 01:01:15.890
I can go in hungry and get
out with no desire to eat.

01:01:15.890 --> 01:01:18.530
I can only speculate.

01:01:18.530 --> 01:01:21.290
The sauna or any kind of
deliberate heat exposure

01:01:21.290 --> 01:01:24.193
that's uncomfortable releases
this molecule dynorphin.

01:01:24.193 --> 01:01:25.610
This is actually
the same molecule

01:01:25.610 --> 01:01:29.900
that's released under conditions
of alcohol withdrawal.

01:01:29.900 --> 01:01:32.430
It makes you feel
agitated and not good.

01:01:32.430 --> 01:01:34.280
And then there's this rebound.

01:01:34.280 --> 01:01:37.400
The way it feels
good is later, it

01:01:37.400 --> 01:01:40.680
causes this upregulation in the
so-called mu opioid receptors.

01:01:40.680 --> 01:01:43.820
So the chemicals that you
have, your so-called endogenous

01:01:43.820 --> 01:01:45.920
opioids, not the
opioids that are

01:01:45.920 --> 01:01:47.150
related to the opioid crisis.

01:01:47.150 --> 01:01:48.920
But the ones that
you naturally make

01:01:48.920 --> 01:01:53.690
are able to have a more
robust effect after the sauna.

01:01:53.690 --> 01:01:56.900
Dynorphin is an
appetite suppressant

01:01:56.900 --> 01:02:01.310
and for reasons related to
kind of general discomfort

01:02:01.310 --> 01:02:02.430
in the body.

01:02:02.430 --> 01:02:04.280
So that's the only
reason I can speculate.

01:02:04.280 --> 01:02:05.697
There are a number
of other things

01:02:05.697 --> 01:02:08.300
that sauna does including
massive increases in growth

01:02:08.300 --> 01:02:11.160
hormone provided you
don't sauna too much.

01:02:11.160 --> 01:02:14.810
So if you do it once a week
four 20-minute sessions spaced

01:02:14.810 --> 01:02:18.260
five minutes apart, you get
these enormous increases

01:02:18.260 --> 01:02:19.080
in growth hormone.

01:02:19.080 --> 01:02:20.750
If you start doing
it more often you

01:02:20.750 --> 01:02:24.290
get still significant,
but smaller increases

01:02:24.290 --> 01:02:26.620
in growth hormone.

01:02:26.620 --> 01:02:29.860
And my team, this is how
the podcast goes too.

01:02:29.860 --> 01:02:31.760
At some point Rob just
goes, it's enough.

01:02:31.760 --> 01:02:34.000
So if you think the
episodes are long now,

01:02:34.000 --> 01:02:35.170
they'd be a lot longer.

01:02:35.170 --> 01:02:37.030
Listen, I just want
to-- before we part,

01:02:37.030 --> 01:02:39.370
I know it's a Sunday night
and people have to go,

01:02:39.370 --> 01:02:41.750
I want to thank everyone
for coming out tonight.

01:02:41.750 --> 01:02:45.620
I know that at least for me
I'm still sort of baffled,

01:02:45.620 --> 01:02:48.730
but pleasantly so that people
are interested in investing

01:02:48.730 --> 01:02:53.240
time to come out and hear
hours of a nerd like me

01:02:53.240 --> 01:02:55.600
and talk about
science and tools.

01:02:55.600 --> 01:02:58.690
And I'm delighted that
people are hopefully gleaning

01:02:58.690 --> 01:02:59.878
some useful information.

01:02:59.878 --> 01:03:01.420
Please do pass along
the information.

01:03:01.420 --> 01:03:03.473
I didn't invent this stuff
as I mentioned before.

01:03:03.473 --> 01:03:05.140
I was not consulted
on the design phase.

01:03:05.140 --> 01:03:06.620
I have no domain over it.

01:03:06.620 --> 01:03:08.320
This is the stuff
of mother nature.

01:03:08.320 --> 01:03:11.980
And whatever other beliefs
you have, they're here in us.

01:03:11.980 --> 01:03:15.070
And of course, I'd be remiss
if I didn't finish by saying,

01:03:15.070 --> 01:03:16.892
have a wonderful
night and thank you

01:03:16.892 --> 01:03:18.100
for your interest in science.

01:03:18.100 --> 01:03:21.450
AUDIENCE: [APPLAUSE]

